levant: §42-§47, §54-§58, §60 O1, §61 O2, §62 O3, §65 O8, §76 O17, §77 O18, §78 O19, §86, §88, §89. The kernel Ern/MM03.lean formalizes an earlier text and must stay unchanged; the new modules are Ern/Derived/Arena.lean and Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean. Rules in /Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern/AGENTS.md (Review section applies to you).\n\nThe task brief (French, summarized faithfully):\nQuestion 1 - derivability of arenas. Hypothesis: with MetaRight restricted to Modify exercisable on the arena value (writeShared branch removed for issue and reset), O17-O19 are realized with only O1-O16 plus abstraction metadata (available, issued, registry).\n * issue_as_kernel: for every reached state, restricted issue is ok iff derive (Modify, from internal, on the region) then store (into the destination) is ok, with the same arrival state on the kernel part; fail iff the region is unavailable in the metadata; reject otherwise. Both directions required. \"verifier que la clause G2 de store (cible encore exercable) et l'option 2 n'introduisent aucun rejet que issue n'a pas, et reciproquement\".\n * reset_as_kernel: restricted reset ok iff derive (Modify, from internal, on the whole storage) then release of it is ok. Prove both clauses of ResetPre follow, the second via release's preconditions.\n * newArena_as_library: registration and guard of section 56 are library metadata ops with no kernel step; establish which safety property, if any, depends on them; expected none, since I1 already rejects two issues on the same region through two administrations.\n * Keep the writeShared counterexample: reached state, Observe access on the arena value, SharedMutable metadata, no active issue; unrestricted reset/issue ok while the O1-O16 sequence is reject.\n * If an equivalence fails for another reason, report the counterexample and clause, without modifying the kernel.\nQuestion 2 - DataStack library instance built on kernel ops and the derived issue/reset. State: storage, internal, top, ordered list of live marks; the stack value holds internal. push size: ok|fail|reject, issue at the top (restricted issue), fail if no room. mark: ok, creates (stack, fresh id, top) added to the list. rewind m: ok|fail|reject; fail if m is not a live mark of this stack; reject if an active authority derived from internal covers a region issued after m; otherwise regions above m return to available, m and all marks above removed, older marks stay live, no initialization changes. grow b extra: ok|fail|reject, in place only; requires b the last issued block with room following; releases b's authority, returns to the top before b, issues a region with same base and larger size; fail if no room. Theorems: rewind_rejects_live_above, mark_no_aba (a removed mark never becomes valid again, even if the top returns to the same position), rewind_keeps_older_marks, grow_same_base, grow_keeps_prefix_init, grow_keeps_prefix_bytes (bytes and provenance of the prefix unchanged, via MM23 without changing it). Cases: several stacks, rewind refused then accepted, ABA, grow ok and grow without room, frame scenario.\nRules: no sorry, axioms only propext/Quot.sound, no noncomputable, no warnings, nothing invented, no weakened statement, counterexamples kept.\n\nReport only findings with concrete evidence (spec quote, counterexample run output, or command output). No evidence, no finding. Do not ask for additions beyond the task. Severity: blocking (a statement or definition says something other than the spec/brief, is vacuous, or is weaker than claimed), major (a deviation or interpretation choice not reported in docstrings, or a missing required item), minor (citation, wording). Cause: formalization | coverage | spec.\n`\n\nconst FINDINGS = {\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: {\n    findings: {\n      type: 'array',\n      items: {\n        type: 'object',\n        properties: {\n          id: { type: 'string' },\n          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocking', 'major', 'minor'] },\n          location: { type: 'string' },\n          cause: { type: 'string', enum: ['formalization', 'coverage', 'spec'] },\n          claim: { type: 'string' },\n          evidence: { type: 'string' },\n        },\n        required: ['id', 'severity', 'location', 'cause', 'claim', 'evidence'],\n      },\n    },\n    summary: { type: 'string' },\n  },\n  required: ['findings', 'summary'],\n}\n\nconst VERDICTS = {\n  type: 'object',\n  properties: {\n    verdicts: {\n      type: 'array',\n      items: {\n        type: 'object',\n        properties: {\n          id: { type: 'string' },\n          real: { type: 'boolean' },\n          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocking', 'major', 'minor'] },\n          reason: { type: 'string' },\n          evidence: { type: 'string' },\n        },\n        required: ['id', 'real', 'severity', 'reason', 'evidence'],\n      },\n    },\n  },\n  required: ['verdicts'],\n}\n\nconst DIMS = [\n  { key: 'issue', prompt: `Focus: question 1, issue. Files: formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean sections \"Restricted issue\", \"The kernel sequence for issue\", \"issue as split (or derive) then store\", and the counterexamples ce_g2, ce_init, ce_avail, ce_empty_region. Check (1) IssueR / IssueStaticR / issueEffR / ArrivalR against the candidate §77 text clause by clause: not stronger, not weaker; note any clause of §77 missing or added. (2) Both directions of issue_to_seq, seq_to_issue, issue_as_kernel_split, issue_as_kernel: are hypotheses satisfiable and minimal, is any statement vacuous or weaker than its docstring claims, is \"the same arrival state\" really stated. (3) Hunt for further deviations between the restricted issue and the sequence split/derive then store, in BOTH directions, beyond the ones the file names (store clause O8.5 HasRight after = ce_g2; issue non-Init = ce_init; availability = ce_avail; empty/non-place region). Try to construct reached-state counterexamples with #eval/decide where the sequence accepts and IssueR rejects with region available and destination not Init, or IssueR accepts and the destination is still exercisable after but the sequence rejects. Check the claim that store's O8.3 owned-storage clause is implied in reached states (ownedIn_holds). (4) Check the kernel IssueStep vs IssueR differences are documented (option 2, anchor in D).` },\n  { key: 'reset-newarena', prompt: `Focus: question 1, reset, newArena and writeShared. Files: formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean sections \"Restricted reset\", \"newArena as a library operation\", and theorems ce_reset_anchor, ce_reset_empty, reset_alloc_ok, ce_writeShared, ce_two_admins, ce_avail_reject. Check (1) ResetPreR against §78 clause by clause; the file reads §78.3 \"sous-arbre d'origines de internal\" as including internal itself (as the kernel ResetPre does): is that an unreported interpretation, and is the consequence (reset impossible while the arena value holding internal through store is usable) correctly shown? (2) reset_to_seq, seq_to_reset, reset_as_kernel: both directions, extra hypotheses exactly the gaps, non-vacuous; the brief asked to prove the second clause of ResetPre follows via release's preconditions - is the file's claim that it does not follow correct? (3) ce_writeShared matches the brief's required counterexample (reached state, Observe access on the arena value, SharedMutable metadata, no active issue, unrestricted reset AND issue ok, sequences reject). (4) newArena_as_library: does ReachableNG really allow the unguarded registration on top of all Reachable steps; does Inv57 cover every invariant listed in §57 (WF3, Inv3 = I1..I4, AnchorOrder, NoDangling, AnchorOrderHeld, NoOrphan, ArenaInv of live arenas); is step_inv57 faithful to the kernel proofs; is the conclusion \"no safety property depends on the guard\" overstated (e.g. HandlePool or library registries, §56's two registries).` },\n  { key: 'datastack-aba', prompt: `Focus: question 2, DataStack, especially mark_no_aba and rewind. File: formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean. Check (1) the definitions of push, mark, rewind, grow, newStack, dexec against the brief's contract (outcomes ok/fail/reject and when each happens, effects); list every choice the brief does not dictate (e.g. static checks before fail, 0 < extra, grow's destination, rewind's region \"above\", \"covers a region issued after m\" read as \"covers a cell above m\", mark requiring no right, blocks kept by filter, KGuard on kernel newArena) and whether its docstring states it. (2) mark_no_aba adversarially: try to make a removed mark valid again through any DOp sequence (newStack, kernel ops, mark, rewind, grow, push), including identical marks in two stacks, fresh counter behaviour; is the hypothesis MarksWF satisfiable and established for reached library states (drun_init_marksWF); is the statement weaker than the brief's (\"never becomes valid again, even if the top returns to the same position\"). (3) rewind_rejects_live_above and its converse: does \"above st m\" capture \"regions issued after m\" in all states, including after grow (a grown block straddling a mark) and after rewind with filter; construct a run where an active authority derived from internal covers a region issued after m but rewind m is ok, or rewind is rejected though nothing derived from internal is active above. (4) rewind_keeps_older_marks: older marks stay, m and above removed, no init change, other stacks unchanged.` },\n  { key: 'datastack-grow-cases', prompt: `Focus: question 2, grow theorems, bytes, reachability and cases. File: formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean (and formal/Ern/MM23.lean for the refinement, unchanged). Check (1) grow_same_base, grow_keeps_prefix_init, grow_keeps_prefix_bytes say what the brief asks and are not vacuous; in grow_keeps_prefix_bytes, is the MM-02 trace of grow correctly identified (release and split are NoStep per MM23, store's counterpart is storeOps), is LayoutWF taken on the right state, is \"bytes and provenance\" covered by rangeBytes; would the theorem hold trivially for a wrong reason. (2) dexec_reach / drun_reach: every ok library step is a sequence of Reachable kernel steps. (3) The cases (case_rewind, case_aba, case_grow, case_stacks, case_frame, cases_reachable): non-vacuous (the run succeeded, isSome stated), and they show what their docstrings claim; check the frame scenario is a real mark-at-entry / rewind-at-exit pattern. (4) Run the done-criteria yourself: cd formal && ~/.elan/bin/lake build (must have no warnings), grep -rnE '\\\\b(sorry|admit|native_decide)\\\\b|^\\\\s*axiom\\\\b' --include='*.lean' --exclude-dir=.lake . from the repo root (must print nothing), and an axiom check of the theorems of both new modules (only propext, Quot.sound allowed; write a scratch file with #print axioms for the main theorems).` },\n]\n\nphase('Review')\nconst results = await pipeline(\n  DIMS,\n  d => agent(COMMON + '\\n' + d.prompt + '\\nReturn your findings.', { label: `review:${d.key}`, phase: 'Review', schema: FINDINGS }),\n  (rev, d) => {\n    if (!rev || !rev.findings || rev.findings.length === 0) return { key: d.key, review: rev, verdicts: [] }\n    return agent(COMMON + `\\nYou are a verifier. Another reviewer reported the findings below on the focus \"${d.key}\". For EACH finding, try hard to REFUTE it: re-read the cited code and spec text, rerun or write the counterexample yourself. Mark real=false if the claim is wrong, already documented in a docstring of the file, outside the brief, or lacks evidence; real=true only if you reproduced or confirmed it. Re-assess severity.\\n\\nFindings:\\n${JSON.stringify(rev.findings, null, 1)}`,\n      { label: `verify:${d.key}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICTS })\n      .then(v => ({ key: d.key, review: rev, verdicts: v ? v.verdicts : null }))\n  },\n)\nreturn results\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
