","slug":"effervescent-gathering-wind"}
{"parentUuid":"8e3cc9de-00fa-4599-9697-327acb24e434","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"04d7bf8f-72b3-4331-acc7-9edddd89b24a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LdZzjNtJyQoiiUunCwXFJY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t=== issue\n2\tSUMMARY: Review of question 1, issue (formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean: restricted issue, the kernel sequence, issue_to_seq / seq_to_issue / issue_as_kernel_split / issue_as_kernel, ce_g2 / ce_init / ce_avail / ce_empty_region).\n3\t\n4\tThe equivalence theorems hold up. `issue_as_kernel_split`, `issue_as_kernel`, `issue_to_seq`, `seq_to_issue` and the four ce_* theorems depend only on propext and Quot.sound. Neither file contains sorry, admit, native_decide, axiom or noncomputable.\n5\t\n6\t- **Same arrival state.** It is really stated: `StoreStep … (.ok s')` uses the s' of `IssueR`, and `seq_to_issue` gives `IssueR … (.ok s₂ _)`. `issueEffR_eq` is a structural equality.\n7\t- **Not vacuous.** On the reached state of opsArena, `IssueR arD [1] 3` is ok and `HasRight` on 3 holds in its arrival state (checked by decide). The hypotheses `hg2`, `hav` and `hni` are each needed: ce_g2, ce_avail and ce_init.\n8\t- **No further deviation can exist.** The iff is machine-checked, so for reached states with `ar ∈ s.arenas` the only gaps between `IssueR` and split/derive then store are O8.5 (`HasRight` on the arrival state), availability and `Init`. The counterexamples the brief asks for (sequence accepts while `IssueR` rejects with the region available and the destination not `Init`, or `IssueR` accepts with the destination still exercisable while the sequence rejects) cannot exist.\n9\t- **O8.3.** The owned-storage clause of store is discharged in reached states by `ownedIn_holds` through `RootLive`.\n10\t- **Kernel versus restricted issue.** The differences are documented: `MetaRightM` (`metaRightM_iff`), destination outside the region (option 2, in the `IssueStaticR` and `issueStaticR_iff` docstrings) and the anchor added to `D dst` (`issueEffR` docstring). The extra `EffAnchorOK` conjunct of `ArrivalR` changes no outcome: `AnchorOrderHeld` on the arrival state already implies it, as the `seq_to_issue` proof uses.\n11\t\n12\tThe findings are about `IssueR` against §77 itself, not about the equivalence: one major and two minor, listed below.\n13\t- Q1I-1 [major->major] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean:97-111 (IssueR, through kernel Avail at Ern/MM03.lean:7104 and the issueEff filter at :7080-7087); docstring of ce_empty_region at :1145-1149\n14\t  CLAIM: `IssueR` reads availability leaf by leaf: a region counts as available when each of its leaves is in `ar.available`. The candidate text gives `available` as a set of regions and tests whether the region belongs to it. The text does not say which reading is meant, and the two readings give different outcomes. This interpretation is reported neither in the docstrings nor in the report. Under the leaf reading, the overlap clause of §77.5 can never be reached in a reached state, a region that partly overlaps an active emission gets `fail` where the region-set reading gives `reject`, and the empty region is always available.\n15\t  EVIDENCE: Spec text. §46 types the field as `available: Set<Region>`; §77.4 says \"Si les conditions statiques tiennent mais que `region` n'appartient plus à `available`, l'opération produit `fail`\". §77.5 sits after the availability check and says \"Si la région est disponible, le mode effectif de `internal` … à défaut, l'issue est `reject`. La région ne peut chevaucher une région émise encore active.\" The effect says \"La région quitte `available`\".\n16\t\n17\tFormal side: kernel `Avail ar region := ∀ l ∈ region, l ∈ ar.available`. The `IssueR` docstring only says \"`fail` … when the region is not available (§77.4)\".\n18\t\n19\tCheck 1 (scratch b.lean, decide): reached state `run K0 [] (opsAlloc ++ [.issue 0 0 [5] 2])`. `reach_run` holds and `arenas = [⟨4, 4, [6], [([5], 5)]⟩]`. Block 5 is `([5], active, origin 4)`. The following typecheck: `IssueR arQ [5, 6] 3 s (.fail s arQ)` and `¬ IssueRight s arQ [5, 6]`. Under a region-set reading where issuing [5] removes only the region [5], [5,6] is still available and §77.5 gives `reject`. Under the leaf reading, ArenaInv clause 4 makes every region that overlaps an active emission unavailable, so the overlap sentence of §77.5 never applies.\n20\t\n21\tCheck 2 (scratch a.lean, decide): state `run K0 [] (opsArena ++ [.issue 0 0 [1] 2])`, whose arena is `⟨1, 4, [], [([1], 5)]⟩` (available empty). `IssueR arIss [] 3 s (.ok …)` typechecks and `Avail arIss [] ` holds, so the empty region is available although `available = []`. ce_empty_region states that this `ok` is acceptable \n22\t  VERDICT: Confirmed. `IssueR` decides `fail` with the kernel's `Avail ar region := ∀ l ∈ region, l ∈ ar.available`. That is a leaf-by-leaf reading, possible only because the kernel `Arena.available` is a `List Nat` of leaves. The candidate text types the field as `Set<Region>` and tests whether the region belongs to it, so the text is open to more than one reading. §78 also says \"la totalité des régions du stockage administré redevient disponible\". The readings classify differently: on a reached state the leaf reading gives `fail` where a set-of-regions reading gives `reject` through §77.5. This choice is stated nowhere: not in the docstrings of `IssueR`/`IssueStaticR`/`ce_empty_region`, not in the kernel `Avail`/`Arena` docstrings, and not in the report's (c) gap list (whose item 7 lists other kernel-vs-candidate differences) or its (f) questions. Earlier reports (MM03 p2–p8) never arbitrate it either. Per AGENTS.md, a choice that changes behavior is a semantic question to report, and the brief explicitly asks for \"fail iff the region is unavailable in the metadata; reject otherwise\".\n23\t  VEVIDENCE: Spec §46: \"available: Set<Region>\"; §77.4: \"que `region` n'appartient plus à `available`, l'opération produit fail\"; §77.5: \"La région ne peut chevaucher une région émise encore active.\"; §78 effect: \"la totalité des régions du stockage administré redevient disponible\". Kernel Ern/MM03.lean:7104 `def Avail (ar : Arena) (region : List Nat) : Prop := ∀ l ∈ region, l ∈ ar.available`. Scratch /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/verify-issue/v1.lean (compiles, no errors): `#eval (run K0 [] (opsAlloc ++ [.issue 0 0 [5] 2])).s.arenas` prints `[(4, 4, [6], [([5], 5)])]`. These hold: `Reachable K0 [] …` via reach_run, `IssueR arQ [5, 6] 3 s (.fail s arQ)` and `¬ IssueRight s arQ [5, 6]`. So the leaf reading gives fail where a set-of-regions reading (only [5] leaves `available`) reaches §77.5 and gives reject. Scratch v2.lean: with arIss = ⟨1,4,[],[([1],5)]⟩ (the reached entry after opsArena ++ [.issue 0 0 [1] 2]), `Avail arIss []` and `IssueR arIss [] 3 s (.ok …)` both typecheck, so the empty region counts as available even though `available = []`. A grep of the report and the Arena.lean docstrings for this reading finds nothing.\n24\t- Q1I-2 [minor->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean:51-63 (IssueStaticR docstring and definition) and :97-111 (IssueR)\n25\t  CLAIM: The first sentence of §77.1, \"L'entrée `arena` existe\", is not a clause of `IssueStaticR` or `IssueR`. Nothing writes the new metadata `ar'` back into the state either. Nothing ties `IssueR` to a registered entry: it accepts any `Arena` value, and its arrival state keeps the stale entry in `s.arenas`. The equivalence theorems are unaffected, since they all assume `har : ar ∈ s.arenas`. There is no restricted counterpart of the kernel's `IssueAt`.\n26\t  EVIDENCE: Spec §77.1: \"L'entrée `arena` existe et est vivante ; `internal` est active.\" The `IssueStaticR` docstring reads clause 1 as \"restricted metadata right and `internal` active\" only.\n27\t\n28\tScratch a.lean, decide:\n29\t- `arFake := ⟨1, 4, [1], [([7], 9)]⟩`. `arFake ∉ (run K0 [] opsArena).s.arenas` holds, yet `IssueR arFake [1] 3 (run K0 [] opsArena).s (.ok …)` typechecks.\n30\t- On `run K0 [] opsAvail`, the registered entry fails (ce_avail), but the made-up value `⟨1, 4, [1], []⟩` gives `IssueR … (.ok …)`.\n31\t- `#eval (issueEffR (run K0 [] opsArena).s arD [1] 3).1.arenas.map (fun a => (a.available, a.issued))` prints `[([1], [])]`: the arrival state still lists [1] as available and nothing as issued.\n32\t  VERDICT: Confirmed. §77.1 begins \"L'entrée `arena` existe\". `IssueStaticR`'s docstring presents clause 1 as \"restricted metadata right and `internal` active\" and drops the existence requirement. `IssueR` takes any `Arena` value and yields `ok` for entries that are not in `s.arenas`. Every equivalence theorem assumes `har : ar ∈ s.arenas`, so no proven statement is affected. That keeps this minor: a definition and docstring coverage gap, not a wrong theorem. The sub-claim about write-back is weaker: `issueEffR` returns the new metadata as the second component, mirroring the kernel `IssueStep`, and the theorems equate it explicitly (`ar' = (issueEffR …).2`). Unlike the kernel's `IssueAt`, though, there is no restricted wrapper that looks up the entry and writes it back.\n33\t  VEVIDENCE: Spec §77.1: \"L'entrée `arena` existe et est vivante ; `internal` est active.\" Arena.lean:51-56 docstring: \"clause 1, restricted metadata right and `internal` active\". Scratch /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/verify-issue/v2.lean compiles: `arFake := ⟨1, 4, [1], [([7], 9)]⟩`, `arFake ∉ (run K0 [] opsArena).s.arenas` by decide, and `IssueR arFake [1] 3 (run K0 [] opsArena).s (.ok …)` via `.ok (by decide) ×4`. Likewise `arMade := ⟨1,4,[1],[]⟩ ∉ (run K0 [] opsAvail).s.arenas`, but `IssueR arMade [1] 3 … (.ok …)`, while the registered entry fails (ce_avail). `#eval (issueEffR (run K0 [] opsArena).s arD [1] 3).1.arenas.map (fun a => (a.available, a.issued))` prints `[([1], [])]`.\n34\t- Q1I-3 [minor->minor] real=False loc=formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean:51-56 (IssueStaticR docstring) and :57-63\n35\t  CLAIM: The static part of §77.3 (\"restrictions statiques de validité et de non-échappement applicables à la destination\") has no counterpart in the formalization. The docstring says these restrictions \"are those of the kernel (`IssueStatic`, `issueStaticR_iff`)\", but `IssueStatic` has no conjunct for clause 3: its eight conjuncts encode §77.1 and §77.2 only. The text does not say what these restrictions are, and reading them as empty is an interpretation that is not flagged as one. This matters for classification: any such restriction is checked before `fail`, so leaving it empty can turn a static `reject` into `fail`. Under the kernel's `NoEscape`, which constrains only root anchors, the block (origin `internal`) is trivially non-escaping, so this may change no outcome. That is not stated or proven.\n36\t  EVIDENCE: Spec §77.3: \"Les restrictions **statiques** de validité et de non-échappement applicables à la destination sont satisfaites. Le contrôle de `AnchorOrder` qui dépend de la nouvelle ancre est effectué sur **l'état d'arrivée simulé** …\".\n37\t\n38\tKernel `IssueStatic` (Ern/MM03.lean:7091-7097) is `MetaRight ∧ Active internal ∧ region ⊆ afp internal ∧ Usable dst ∧ HasRight dst ∧ dst not Init ∧ isLeaf dst ∧ dst holds no active authority`. Its docstring cites only \"MM-03.3 §6; A10 … J1\".\n39\t\n40\t`grep -n 'NoEscape' Ern/MM03.lean` finds it used in transfer (:1251, :1274) and depend (:1308), never in issue. The report does not list this reading among its questions.\n41\t  VERDICT: Refuted. The docstring already documents the reading: \"The static restrictions of clause 3 are those of the kernel (`IssueStatic`, `issueStaticR_iff`); its `AnchorOrder` check is on the arrival state (`ArrivalR`).\" §77.3 never defines these restrictions, and the finding offers no outcome that changes. The only non-escape restriction the kernel has, `NoEscape`, constrains only anchors with no origin. The issued block always has origin `internal`, so `NoEscape` of the new block holds for every state, entry, region, destination and frame: adding it cannot turn any `fail` into `reject` or change any other outcome. The escape-related anchor checks of §77.3 and §77.6 are formalized in `ArrivalR` on the arrival state, as the text requires. The finding itself concedes \"this may change no outcome\". With no counterexample and a documented reading, there is no finding.\n42\t  VEVIDENCE: Arena.lean:55-56: \"The static restrictions of clause 3 are those of the kernel (`IssueStatic`, `issueStaticR_iff`); its `AnchorOrder` check is on the arrival state (`ArrivalR`).\" Kernel Ern/MM03.lean:723: `def NoEscape (s : S3) (x : Nat) (fd : Option Nat) : Prop := OptP (s.A[x]?) (fun y => y.origin = none → …) True`. Scratch /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/verify-issue/v2.lean proves, with no error: `theorem noEscape_block (s : S3) (ar : Arena) (region : List Nat) (dst : Nat) (fd : Option Nat) : NoEscape (issueEffR s ar region dst).1 s.A.length fd := by simp [NoEscape, issueEffR, issueEff, S3.addAuth, OptP]`. A kernel non-escape check on the destination is therefore vacuous for issue.\n43\t=== reset-newarena\n44\tSUMMARY: Verdict: changes (two minor findings, no blocking or major). All checks were by Lean runs against the built modules. Scratch files are in /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/reset/ (T1–T5.lean). No repository file was modified.\n45\t\n46\t(1) ResetPreR against §78, clause by clause. MetaRightM is the first branch of §78.1. Clause 2 (`internal ∈ ancs j → ¬Active j`) is §78.2. Clause 3 reads §78.3 with `internal` included. That reading is not silent: the ResetPreR and reset_clause3_strict docstrings and the report (f)1 all name it, and the report flags the ambiguity for arbitration. The consequence is correctly shown, by ce_reset_anchor and reset_blocked_by_stored_internal. On the opsArena state, MetaRightM, §78.2 and the strict form of clause 3 all hold, so only the inclusive part (`4 ∈ D 0`) blocks the reset. After `transfer 0 2`, the anchor follows to D 2 and the reset stays blocked. The one deviation is that ResetPreR omits §78.1's liveness and existence (F1).\n47\t\n48\t(2) The reset equivalence is proven in both directions and is not vacuous: reset_alloc_ok holds on a reached live arena. hemp and hanc are exactly the gaps:\n49\t- ce_reset_empty: MetaRightM and clause 3 hold, so only hemp fails.\n50\t- ce_reset_anchor: clause 2 holds, so only hanc fails.\n51\t\n52\tThe brief expected clause 3 to follow from release's preconditions. The file says it does not, and that is correct: ReleasePre (MM03.lean:1004) only checks anchors equal to the released authority itself, which is fresh. ce_reset_anchor has ReleaseStep ok while clause 3 fails. hact is an extra hypothesis that comes from F1, not from the spec. Only one reading of §78.3 is covered (F2).\n53\t\n54\t(3) ce_writeShared matches the brief. It has:\n55\t- a reached state (a6_reachable);\n56\t- `issued = []`;\n57\t- the kernel issue and reset ok;\n58\t- ¬MetaRightM;\n59\t- derive and split of internal on the region, and derive on the storage, all rejected;\n60\t- IssueR rejected and ¬ResetPreR.\n61\t\n62\tTwo required items are not conjuncts of the theorem, but I checked they hold. The Observe access: a6.A[4] is Observe on [0], origin 0, and a6.top = 2. The SharedMutable metadata: metaLeaves a6 ar7 = [0] and KA marks leaf 0. The issue under the candidate text with the full MetaRight is also ok in a6 (IssueStatic, 6 ∉ [4], Avail, IssueRight, ArrivalR), and so is ResetPre KA a6 ar7 4.\n63\t\n64\t(4) newArena_as_library:\n65\t- ReachableNG has init, every Step (all Reachable constructors, by reachableNG_of_reachable) and the unguarded newArenaNG.\n66\t- newArenaPre_iff shows NewArenaPreNG is exactly the kernel precondition minus the guard.\n67\t- Inv57 plus the conclusion covers the whole §57 conjunction: WF3, Inv3 (I1–I4, AnchorOrder, NoDangling), AnchorOrderHeld, NoOrphan via RootLive, and ArenaInv of every live entry.\n68\t- step_inv57 and newArenaNG_inv57 compile from Inv57 alone, without reachability.\n69\t- ce_two_admins is a non-vacuous ReachableNG state: two live entries share internal 4, and the double issue is rejected through the effective right.\n70\t\n71\tI found no evidence that the \"no safety property depends on the guard\" conclusion is overstated:\n72\t- The kernel has no theorem that uses the guard clause.\n73\t- The DataStack proofs do not use Guard56 or KGuard.\n74\t- HandlePool's PReach already allows a pool and an arena sharing internal 4, and two pools sharing internal 4 (T2: `([4],[4])` and `([4,4],[])`), and its pool theorems are proven over PReach.\n75\t\n76\tThe theorem's own docstring correctly limits the claim to §57. The §56 uniqueness property fails trivially without the guard, which ce_two_admins shows.\n77\t\n78\tAxioms: all the theorems in focus depend only on propext and Quot.sound. The sorry/admit/native_decide/axiom/noncomputable grep on Arena.lean is empty.\n79\t\n80\tNot checked: the issue-side theorems and DataStack, which were outside this review's focus.\n81\t- F1 [minor->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean:659-667 (ResetPreR), used by reset_to_seq/seq_to_reset/reset_as_kernel (714-803)\n82\t  CLAIM: ResetPreR leaves out the first sentence of §78.1, \"L'entrée existe et est vivante.\" It is therefore more permissive than §78. It is also inconsistent with IssueStaticR, which does ask `s.Active ar.internal` (line 58). reset_as_kernel makes up for this with the section hypotheses `har` and `hact`. The docstrings name only hemp/hanc as the gaps of the equivalence. `hact` is not a spec gap: it is needed only because the definition drops liveness. With liveness in the definition, both sides would imply it (DerivePre asks `s.Active src`). The ResetPreR docstring presents the definition as the §78 preconditions and does not mention the omission. No stated theorem is false, because every consumer carries hact.\n83\t  EVIDENCE: Scratch run (/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/reset/T1.lean), all by decide on opsDead := opsArena ++ [.release 4]: `Reachable K0 [] (run K0 [] opsDead).s` (reach_run), `arenas = [arD]`, `¬ Active 4`, `ResetPreR (run K0 [] opsDead).s arD` HOLDS, while `ResetAtStep K0 0 0 _ .reject`, `DeriveStep 4 1 .modify _ .reject` and `IssueR arD [1] 2 _ .reject`. So on a reached state with a dead registered entry, ResetPreR accepts. The kernel reset, the derive+release sequence and the restricted issue all reject. Spec §78.1: \"L'entrée existe et est vivante.\"\n84\t  VERDICT: Reproduced. ResetPreR (Arena.lean:659-667) has only three conjuncts: MetaRightM, §78.2, and inclusive §78.3. It drops the first sentence of §78.1, \"L'entrée existe et est vivante.\" Its docstring presents it as the \"Preconditions of the restricted `reset` (candidate Spec 1 §78...)\" and cites §78.1 only for the metadata right. No docstring says the definition leaves liveness out. The theorem docstrings do limit their domain (\"for a live arena\" on reset_to_seq and reset_as_kernel). Every positive use of ResetPreR carries the hypotheses har/hact or sits on a live arena (reset_alloc_ok), so no stated theorem is false. That makes this a wording/citation issue, not a blocking one. Mitigation: the omission copies the kernel, where ResetPre has no liveness clause and ResetAtPre adds it, while IssueStatic does include Active. It is still a definition more permissive than §78, and its docstring does not say so.\n85\t  VEVIDENCE: I ran /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/verify-reset/V1.lean through lake env lean. Every example passed with no error; a sanity check in V0.lean shows a false decide does report an error. With opsDead := opsArena ++ [.release 4], the following all hold: `Reachable K0 [] (run K0 [] opsDead).s`, `arenas = [arD]`, `¬ Active 4`, `ResetPreR (run K0 [] opsDead).s arD`, `ResetAtStep K0 0 0 _ .reject`, `DeriveStep 4 1 .modify _ .reject`, `IssueR arD [1] 2 _ .reject`. Spec §78.1: \"L'entrée existe et est vivante.\" ResetPreR docstring: \"1 restricted metadata right (§78.1)\", with no mention of liveness.\n86\t- F2 [minor->minor] real=False loc=formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean:721-731 (reset_to_seq), 805-813 (reset_clause3_strict)\n87\t  CLAIM: The §78.3 ambiguity (\"sous-arbre d'origines de internal\", inclusive or strict) is reported as open (ResetPreR docstring; report (f)1). The brief's equivalence, however, is stated only under the inclusive reading. reset_to_seq takes the inclusive clause 3 as a hypothesis, but its proof discards that clause (`obtain ⟨hm, h2, -⟩ := h`). As a result, neither direction of the strict-reading equivalence (strict ResetPre ↔ derive;release ok ∧ hemp) can be obtained from the stated theorems. reset_clause3_strict only gives strict clause 3 from clause 2. If arbitration picks reading (A) of report (e)4, no reset_as_kernel theorem covers it.\n88\t  EVIDENCE: Line 727 of reset_to_seq: `obtain ⟨hm, h2, -⟩ := h` (clause 3 unused). Scratch T2.lean, by decide on the reached state run K0 [] opsArena: `ResetPreStrict s arD ∧ ¬ ResetPreR s arD`, where ResetPreStrict = MetaRightM ∧ §78.2 ∧ (∀ usable q, ∀ x ∈ D q, internal ∉ ancs x). On this state reset_to_seq cannot be applied, although its conclusion holds (ce_reset_anchor: DeriveStep 4 1 ok, ReleaseStep ok).\n89\t  VERDICT: The facts are right: reset_to_seq discards clause 3 at line 727, and the opsArena state satisfies the strict reading but not ResetPreR. They do not make a finding, for three reasons. (1) The reading is not chosen silently. The ResetPreR docstring says \"internal itself included, as the kernel reads §78.3\". reset_clause3_strict's docstring states the strict reading. ce_reset_anchor documents this same state: rejected by clause 3 under the kernel reading, while derive then release are ok. AGENTS asks that an ambiguity be reported, and it was. (2) The brief asks for one reset_as_kernel equivalence. It does not ask for one per reading of an ambiguous passage. Asking for a strict-reading equivalence, in case arbitration later picks reading (A), is an addition beyond the task. (3) reset_to_seq's statement matches the brief (restricted reset implies the sequence is ok). A hypothesis the proof does not use makes the theorem less general than it could be. That does not make it vacuous or weaker than claimed.\n90\t  VEVIDENCE: I confirmed `sed -n 725,728p`: line 727 is `obtain ⟨hm, h2, -⟩ := h`. V2.lean (`ResetPreStrict (run K0 [] opsArena).s arD ∧ ¬ ResetPreR (run K0 [] opsArena).s arD` by decide) compiles. This is the ce_reset_anchor state, already documented. ResetPreR docstring (Arena.lean:659-663): \"3 no usable place with an anchor in the subtree of origins of `internal`, `internal` itself included, as the kernel reads §78.3\". reset_clause3_strict docstring: \"Under the strict reading of §78.3, the clause follows from §78.2 and I3 in reached states.\"\n91\t=== datastack-aba\n92\tSUMMARY: I reviewed formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean against the brief (Q2) and Spec 1 candidate §56, §58, §62, §65, §77 and §78, read-only. Each claim was checked with #eval/decide runs from DS.init under K0. Scratch files: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/ds2/t1.lean to t6.lean. #print axioms for mark_no_aba, rewind_rejects_live_above, rewind_keeps_older_marks and case_aba lists only propext and Quot.sound.\n93\t\n94\t(1) Choices the brief leaves open, and whether a docstring states them:\n95\t- Static checks before fail: stated for push (PushStatic) and grow, but PushStatic omits dst ∉ region (DS-5).\n96\t- 0 < extra: stated.\n97\t- grow's destination: stated vaguely as 'the destination of the block'; it is the recorded dst, not the current holder (DS-8).\n98\t- `above` region: stated, with a false justification (DS-2).\n99\t- 'covers a region issued after m' read as 'covers a cell in [m.top, st.top)': stated in the theorem docstring, but the claimed coincidence is false (DS-2).\n100\t- mark requires no right: implicit; a nonexistent stack gives reject for mark and rewind, not stated in docstrings (DS-6).\n101\t- Blocks kept by the filter: docstring inaccurate (DS-7).\n102\t- KGuard on kernel newArena: stated.\n103\t- push/grow use the store-based derived issue instead of the restricted issue: not stated in docstrings, and the report wrongly calls it representation-only (DS-4).\n104\t- push of size 0: not stated (DS-3).\n105\t\n106\t(2) mark_no_aba is sound, and I found no DOp sequence that makes the rewound mark valid again. Fresh ids are global, the stack index is part of the mark, and stacks are never removed. MarksWF is satisfiable and is established for library runs by drun_init_marksWF. However, the statement covers only the mark passed to rewind, not the later marks removed with it, and mark uniqueness is never proved (DS-1, blocking).\n107\t\n108\t(3) `above` does not capture 'regions issued after m'. After a grow that straddles a mark, or after an intermediate rewind to a later mark, an active authority derived from internal can cover part of a post-m region and rewind m is still ok (DS-2). An empty-footprint block also passes (DS-3). In the other direction, rewind rejects for missing rights with nothing derived above, and the theorem called 'converse' is only the contrapositive (DS-6).\n109\t\n110\t(4) rewind_keeps_older_marks proves what it states: marks = pre, top = m.top, other stacks unchanged, I unchanged. That 'later marks are removed' depends on DS-1's missing uniqueness.\n111\t\n112\tNot checked: grow_keeps_prefix_bytes in depth, and the Q1 module beyond the definitions DataStack uses.\n113\t- DS-1 [blocking->blocking] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:690-711 (mark_no_aba); also 503-517 (rewind_keeps_older_marks), 576-577 (MarksWF)\n114\t  CLAIM: mark_no_aba is weaker than the brief and than its own docstring. It concludes only about the mark passed to rewind. A rewind also removes every later mark of the stack, and no theorem says those marks never become valid again. The brief asks this for any removed mark: 'une marque retirée ne redevient jamais valide'. Nothing establishes that marks are unique either. MarksWF only states m.stack = i and m.id < fresh, so 'later marks removed' in rewind_keeps_older_marks (st'.marks = pre) holds only under an unproven pre/post disjointness.\n115\t  EVIDENCE: Statement (#check): `MarksWF d → rewind k d j m = Out.ok d₁ → drun k d₁ os = some d' → (∀ i st, d'.stacks[i]? = some st → ¬m ∈ st.marks) ∧ ∀ st, d'.stacks[j]? = some st → rewind k d' j m = Out.fail`, which is about `m` only. Docstring: 'a mark removed by `rewind` is live in no stack in any later state'. Run e6 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .push 0 1 5, .mark 0, .kernel (.release 6), .rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩] is ok, and its final stacks are [(0, [], [])]. So ⟨0,1,1⟩ is removed by rewinding ⟨0,0,0⟩, and mark_no_aba says nothing about it. `grep -n Gone` shows Gone is established only for the rewound mark (line 698).\n116\t  VERDICT: Confirmed. mark_no_aba covers only the mark passed to rewind. The brief says 'une marque retirée ne redevient jamais valide', and the brief's own rewind rule says that m and all marks above it are removed, so the later marks are removed marks as well. No theorem covers them. Gone is established only for m (line 698), and no uniqueness invariant on marks exists (grep finds no Nodup or uniqueness lemma). MarksWF does not imply uniqueness: a MarksWF state with a duplicated later mark keeps that mark live after rewind. The theorem is therefore weaker than its own docstring and than report (d), which both say 'a mark removed by rewind is live in no stack'. The docstring of rewind_keeps_older_marks says 'm and the later marks are removed', and that also needs the same unproven uniqueness. In reachable states the property probably holds, but nothing proves it.\n117\t  VEVIDENCE: #check @mark_no_aba gives a conclusion about `m` only: `(∀ i st, d'.stacks[i]? = some st → ¬m ∈ st.marks) ∧ ∀ st, d'.stacks[j]? = some st → rewind k d' j m = Out.fail`. Reproduced E6 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .push 0 1 5, .mark 0, .kernel (.release 6), .rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩]: ok, stacks [(0, [], [])], so ⟨0,1,1⟩ is removed without being the rewound mark. Scratch v2/v3: dDup = final setup1 with stack-0 marks [⟨0,1,0⟩, ⟨0,0,0⟩, ⟨0,1,0⟩] and fresh 2. The MarksWF check by List.all gives `true`. `rewind K0 dDup 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩` gives (\"ok\", [(0, [], [{stack := 0, id := 1, top := 0}])]): the later mark ⟨0,1,0⟩ is still live. So the hypotheses of mark_no_aba cannot yield the claim for later marks.\n118\t- DS-2 [major->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:74-76 (above), 167-185 (rewind), 481-501 (rewind_rejects_live_above, rewind_ok_no_live_above)\n119\t  CLAIM: `above st m` = cells [m.top, st.top) does not capture 'regions issued after m'. The docstrings of `above` and rewind_rejects_live_above say 'the regions issued after m lie there', and two reached runs show this is false. In each run, an active authority derived from internal covers part of a region issued after m, yet rewind m is ok. The report (f) 5 describes the reading as 'couvre une cellule au-dessus du sommet de m'. The code is narrower than that: it also stops at the current top (run E8).\n120\t  EVIDENCE: E4 = setup1 ++ [.push 0 2 5, .mark 0, .grow 0 6 1, .kernel (.release 7), .kernel (.derive 5 2 .modify)]. drun ok; stacks [(3,[⟨0,3,7,5⟩],[⟨0,0,2⟩])]; afp 7 = [2,3,4], the region issued after the mark. A[8] = (active true, origin 5, fp [2]), ancs 8 = [5,1]. dexec rewind 0 ⟨0,0,2⟩ = \"ok\". E8 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .push 0 1 5, .mark 0, .push 0 1 6, .kernel (.release 7), .rewind 0 ⟨0,1,1⟩, .kernel (.release 6), .kernel (.derive 5 3 .modify)]. Cell 3 is block 7's region, issued after ⟨0,0,0⟩. A[9] = (true, some 5, [3]), ancs [5,1]. rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩ = \"ok\", and the next push 0 2 7 = \"reject\". Brief: 'reject si une autorité active dérivée de internal couvre une région émise après m'.\n121\t  VERDICT: The runs reproduce, and the docstring justification is false. The docstrings of `above` and rewind_rejects_live_above say 'the regions issued after m lie there'. E4 contradicts this: the grown region [2,3,4] (block ⟨0,3,7,5⟩, issued by grow after mark ⟨0,0,2⟩) does not lie in above = [4]. The report (f) 5 describes the implemented reading as 'couvre une cellule au-dessus du sommet de m'. E8 contradicts that too: the code takes only [m.top, current top), and authority 9 covers cell 3, which is above m's top, yet rewind is ok. I downgrade to minor. The interpretation question is reported for arbitration in (f) 5. The definition of `above` and its docstring ('those that rewind m returns') state the real bound. Both behaviors are safety-neutral: in E4 the covered cell 2 is not returned by the rewind, and in E8 cell 3 was already available above the top. What is wrong is wording and the report's description, not an unreported deviation.\n122\t  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1. E4: drun ok, stacks [(3, [⟨0,3,7,5⟩], [⟨0,0,2⟩])], A[8] = (true, some 5, [2]), ancs 8 = [5,1], A[7] = (false, some 5, [2,3,4]); `rewind 0 ⟨0,0,2⟩` = \"ok\", and after it stacks = [(2, [⟨0,3,7,5⟩], [])]. E8: drun ok, stacks [(1, [⟨0,1,6,5⟩], [⟨0,0,0⟩])], A[9] = (true, some 5, [3]), ancs [5,1]; `rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩` = \"ok\"; then push 0 2 7 = \"reject\" and push 0 1 7 = \"ok\". Report (f) 5: '« Couvre une région émise après m » est lu comme « couvre une cellule au-dessus du sommet de m »'.\n123\t- DS-3 [major->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:141-156 (push), 171-185 (rewind)\n124\t  CLAIM: push accepts size 0, and neither a docstring nor the report states this choice. The size-0 block is an active authority derived from internal with an empty footprint. rewind of an earlier mark is then ok while that authority stays active, and rewind drops it from `blocks`. This is the empty-footprint gap that Q1 reports for reset (ce_reset_empty, (e) 5). DataStack's rewind inherits it, and neither the DataStack docstrings nor report (d)/(f) mention it.\n125\t  EVIDENCE: E3 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .push 0 0 5]: drun ok. dexec rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩ = \"ok\". After the rewind, stacks = [(0, [], [])] while A[6] = some (true, some 5, []) and ancs 6 = [5,1]: an active derived block that the metadata no longer tracks. Brief: reject when 'une autorité active dérivée de internal couvre une région émise après m'. The region issued after m is [], which authority 6 covers.\n126\t  VERDICT: The run reproduces, but part of the claim is wrong. Accepting push of size 0 is not an undocumented choice. Push is the restricted issue, and the restricted issue accepts the empty region (ce_empty_region: 'the restricted issue of the empty region is ok'). The rewind verdict follows the rule stated in rewind's docstring ('rejected when an active authority derived from internal covers one of them', meaning the cells above m) and the reading reported in (f) 5. What remains confirmed and undocumented is a consequence. An active, empty-footprint block issued after m does not block rewind and is dropped from `blocks` while still active. A reading in which the block's own authority covers its issued region would reject this, as the brief's literal text would. Neither the DataStack docstrings nor (d)/(f) tie this to the empty-footprint gap that Q1 reports for reset ((c) 4, (e) 5, (f) 3). This is minor: the gap is a consequence of a documented reading, and an empty footprint has no safety effect.\n127\t  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1. E3 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .push 0 0 5]: drun ok, stacks [(0, [⟨0,0,6,5⟩], [⟨0,0,0⟩])]; `rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩` = \"ok\". After the rewind, stacks = [(0, [], [])], A[6] = some (true, some 5, []), ancs 6 = [5,1]. Arena.lean:1146-1153 ce_empty_region: IssueR of [] is ok, so push 0 matches the restricted issue.\n128\t- DS-4 [major->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:97-108 (IssueSeq), 139-156 (push), 187-209 (grow); module docstring 10-18\n129\t  CLAIM: The brief asks push to issue at the top through the restricted issue. push and grow instead use the kernel sequence split then store, which Q1 proved differs from IssueR (ce_init, ce_g2). As a result push accepts an Init destination, which §77.2 forbids. No docstring says that push or grow depart from the restricted issue. Report (f) lists this under 'Décisions de représentation … sans effet sur la question posée', but it changes accepted programs.\n130\t  EVIDENCE: E1 = setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5, .kernel (.release 6)]: drun ok and (final e1).s.I 5 == .init is true. dexec push 0 1 5 = \"ok\". §77.2: 'destination est une feuille utilisable, non Init'. AGENTS.md: 'A choice that does [change an accepted program] is a semantic question: stop and report.'\n131\t  VERDICT: Confirmed that push and grow use the kernel sequence split+store and therefore accept an Init destination, which §77.2 forbids for the restricted issue. The deviation and its effect are documented, though. The module docstring and the IssueSeq docstring state that push and grow are split then store. Report (f) says 'Ils acceptent une destination Init, ce que grow exige'. Report (d) says 'IssueR le rejetterait (écart 2)'. The brief also asks for Q2 to be built 'on kernel ops and the derived issue'. What is left is a mislabel: the report files the choice under 'Décisions de représentation … sans effet sur la question posée', although it changes accepted programs (AGENTS.md: such a choice is a semantic question). Because the effect itself is reported, this is minor, not major.\n132\t  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1. E1 = setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5, .kernel (.release 6)]: drun ok, (final e1).s.I 5 == .init = true, and `push 0 1 5` = \"ok\". §77.2: « destination est une feuille utilisable, non Init ». Report (f): « DataStack.push et grow utilisent l'issue dérivé, sémantique de store. Ils acceptent une destination Init, ce que grow exige. »\n133\t- DS-5 [minor->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:87-92 (PushStatic), 145-155\n134\t  CLAIM: PushStatic says it gathers the static preconditions checked before the room, but it omits §77.2 'destination not in region', which the spec orders before the availability fail. A destination inside the requested top region therefore gives fail when room is short, and reject when room is enough.\n135\t  EVIDENCE: In final setup1, PushStatic s st 2 holds and 2 ∈ cellsOf st 0 4 (decide: true; cells [2,3,4]). dexec push 0 4 2 = \"fail\", while dexec push 0 3 2 = \"reject\". §77.2: 'la feuille destination n'appartient pas à region … Cette condition est statique et est vérifiée avant le fail de disponibilité.'\n136\t  VERDICT: Confirmed. PushStatic and the static clause list in report (f) ('par analogie avec §77') omit the §77.2 static clause that the destination is outside the region. That clause must be checked before the availability fail. In push, the check arrives only through store's O8.3, which runs after the room check. A destination inside the requested top region therefore gives fail when room is short and reject when room is enough. The restricted issue would reject in both cases. Neither the docstring nor the report mentions this. It follows from the documented choice of the sequence (DS-4) and changes only a fail/reject classification, so minor.\n137\t  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1: on final setup1, PushStatic s st 2 = true and cellsOf st 0 4 = [2,3,4]; push 0 4 2 = \"fail\", push 0 3 2 = \"reject\". Scratch v2 variant: after setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5], push 0 3 3 = \"fail\" and push 0 2 3 = \"reject\". §77.2: « la feuille destination n'appartient pas à region … Cette condition est statique et est vérifiée avant le fail de disponibilité. »\n138\t- DS-6 [minor->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:167-173 (rewind docstring and none branch), 493-501 (rewind_ok_no_live_above)\n139\t  CLAIM: The rewind docstring gives one reject reason, a covering derived authority, and says fail for a mark that is not live. The code also rejects (a) when the current frame cannot exercise internal at Modify, with nothing derived from internal active, and (b) for a nonexistent stack, where the brief says fail ('fail si m n'est pas une marque vivante de cette pile'). rewind_ok_no_live_above is called the 'Converse' but is the contrapositive of rewind_rejects_live_above. The actual converse is false (run E5).\n140\t  EVIDENCE: E5 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .kernel (.derive 0 0 .observe)]: the active authorities and their ancs are 0..4:[], 5:[1], 6:[0], so nothing descends from 5. above = []. dexec rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩ = \"reject\", while mark 0 = \"ok\". dexec (final setup1) (.rewind 3 ⟨3,0,0⟩) = \"reject\".\n141\t  VERDICT: Partly confirmed. The docstring of rewind_ok_no_live_above and report (d) ('réciproque') present it as the converse of rewind_rejects_live_above. It is the contrapositive restricted to ok, and the real converse is false. In E5 the mark is live and no authority derived from internal covers any cell above it (above = []), yet rewind rejects, because the frame can no longer exercise internal at Modify (O2.1 of the split). The rewind docstring names only the covering-authority reject. The nonexistent-stack reject is documented in report (f) ('une pile inexistante donne reject'), so that part is not a finding. Wording only, so minor.\n142\t  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1. E5 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .kernel (.derive 0 0 .observe)]: drun ok; the active authorities with their ancs are 0-4 [], 5 (origin 1) [1], 6 (origin 0, Observe on [0]) [0]; above st ⟨0,0,0⟩ = []; `rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩` = \"reject\", while `mark 0` = \"ok\". Also `rewind 3 ⟨3,0,0⟩` on final setup1 = \"reject\".\n143\t- DS-7 [minor->minor] real=False loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:167-170, 182\n144\t  CLAIM: The rewind docstring says 'the blocks above m are forgotten'. The filter (·.base < m.top) keeps a grown block that straddles m, so after the rewind the metadata holds a block extending above the new top.\n145\t  EVIDENCE: E4 followed by rewind 0 ⟨0,0,2⟩ gives stacks [(2, [⟨0,3,7,5⟩], [])]: top 2, and a kept block of base 0 and size 3 covering cell index 2 above m.\n146\t  VERDICT: The run reproduces, but the docstring is not contradicted and the rule is documented. A grown block whose base lies below m.top straddles m; it is not 'above m', so keeping it does not contradict 'the blocks above m are forgotten'. Report (f) states the filter explicitly: 'les blocs conservés sont ceux dont la base est sous le sommet de m'. Report (f) 5 notes that a grown block can straddle the top of m. A kept straddling block is also necessarily inactive. Its authority descends from internal and covers a cell in [m.top, top), so an active one makes rewind reject (rewind_rejects_live_above). The stale size has no safety effect.\n147\t  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1: E4b gives stacks [(2, [⟨0,3,7,5⟩], [])] with A[7] = (false, some 5, [2,3,4]), so the kept block is inactive. DataStack.lean:182 `blocks := st.blocks.filter (·.base < m.top)`. Report (f): « les blocs conservés sont ceux dont la base est sous le sommet de m ».\n148\t- DS-8 [minor->minor] real=False loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:187-206 (grow)\n149\t  CLAIM: grow stores the grown block into the destination recorded at push (x.dst), not into the place that now holds b. After a kernel transfer of the block value, grow releases b from its new holder and stores the new block into the old, Moved place. The docstring phrase 'the destination of the block' does not make this clear, and report (f) does not list grow's destination among the decisions.\n150\t  EVIDENCE: E7 = setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5, .kernel (.transfer 5 6)]: A[6] is active and held by place 6, while blocks record dst 5. After .grow 0 6 1, the run is ok, A[7] (fp [2,3]) is held by place 5, and A[6] is inactive and held by 6.\n151\t  VERDICT: The run reproduces, but the behavior is documented and the brief does not fix the destination of grow. The grow docstring says 'into the destination of the block', where Blk.dst is the destination recorded at issue. grow_same_base says the new block is 'held by the same destination' and its statement names x.dst. Report (d) says 'vers la même destination'. Storing into a Moved leaf is allowed by §77.2 ('elle peut notamment être Uninit ou Moved'), and the brief's 'in place only' concerns the region (same base), not the holder.\n152\t  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1. E7 = setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5, .kernel (.transfer 5 6)]: ok, A[6] active and held by place 6, blocks dst 5. After .grow 0 6 1: ok, stacks [(2, [⟨0,2,7,5⟩], [])], A[7] = (true, held by 5, [2,3]), A[6] = (false, held by 6). DataStack.lean:187-190 docstring: « the derived issue of the region of the same base and of size size + extra into the destination of the block ».\n153\t=== datastack-grow-cases\n154\tSUMMARY: Three findings in question 2: two major, one minor. Everything else I was asked to check passed.\n155\t\n156\tDone criteria, run myself:\n157\t- `lake build` succeeds (\"Build completed successfully (12 jobs)\"). Compiling Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean and Ern/Derived/Arena.lean directly with `lake env lean` gives no warnings.\n158\t- The sorry/admit/native_decide/axiom grep prints nothing, and there is no `noncomputable` and no `set_option`.\n159\t- `#print axioms` gives only [propext, Quot.sound] for the main theorems of both modules (dexec_reach, drun_reach, rewind_*, mark_no_aba, grow_*, all case_* and cases_reachable, issue_as_kernel(_split), reset_as_kernel, newArena_as_library).\n160\t- MM03, MM23, HandlePool and Horrors are unchanged since 7d24495.\n161\t\n162\tFindings:\n163\t- **grow ordering (major).** grow returns `fail` for lack of room before it checks the static clauses of the issue it performs (MetaRightM, §77.1). In one reached state with no right on the stack value, grow gives `reject` with room and `fail` without, while push gives `reject` (§77.4, §58).\n164\t- **Derived issue instead of restricted issue (major).** push and grow use split+store instead of the restricted issue named in the brief. push therefore accepts an Init destination that §77.2 and IssueR reject, and grow in place depends on this. The report calls this a representation decision \"sans effet\", although it changes which programs are accepted.\n165\t- **grow_same_base wording (minor).** Its docstring says more than the statement proves: b is shown inactive only in the intermediate state, and \"same destination\" is shown only in the metadata.\n166\t\n167\tWhat checked out:\n168\t1. **The grow theorems.**\n169\t   - grow_keeps_prefix_bytes reads the MM-02 trace correctly: release and split map to [] in MM23.assoc and are NoStep, and store maps to storeOps π.pe s2 π.L s3 a p with s3 the pre-store state (growMid).\n170\t   - LayoutWF depends only on P, which release, split and store leave unchanged, so taking it on d.s is equivalent. Taking it with an arbitrary s2 rather than Repr makes the theorem more general.\n171\t   - rangeBytes returns AByte values that carry provenance, so \"bytes and provenance\" is covered.\n172\t   - The proof rests on the right reason: dst is not in the new region (O8.3, from StorePre) plus LayoutWF.disjoint.\n173\t   - It is not vacuous. In scratch C.lean I applied the theorem to the case_grow state with an explicit layout and MM-02 state (LayoutWF decided). Prefix cells 2 and 3 are unchanged and destination 5 is rewritten (45 becomes 7).\n174\t   - grow_keeps_prefix_init and grow_same_base are non-vacuous (case_grow).\n175\t2. **Reachability.** dexec_reach and drun_reach are correct: push is split+store, rewind is split+release, grow is release+split+store, and kernel steps go through kexec_step / reachable_step. kexec covers only kernel ops.\n176\t3. **Cases.** Every case states that its run succeeded (isSome) and is decided.\n177\t   - The case_rewind rejection comes from SplitPre being false on above = [4].\n178\t   - case_frame is a real mark-at-entry / rewind-at-exit pattern: the mark is taken right after enter, endUse 8 deactivates block 8, rewind runs in frame 2, and exit returns to frame 1 with an empty stack.\n179\t   - case_aba, case_grow and case_stacks show what their docstrings say. The stack-1 rewind in case_stacks is over an empty region, which matches its docstring.\n180\t\n181\tAll scratch files are under /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/q2grow/ (A.lean axioms, B.lean grow ordering, C.lean non-vacuity, D.lean case inspection, E.lean/F.lean Init destination).\n182\t\n183\tNot checked: rewind_rejects_live_above, mark_no_aba, rewind_keeps_older_marks and Q1, beyond running their axioms.\n184\t- Q2-GROW-ORDER [major->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:191-209 (grow), compared with push :141-156 and PushStatic :87-92\n185\t  CLAIM: grow decides `fail` (no room) before it checks the static clauses of the issue it performs: MetaRightM (§77.1) and that the current frame can exercise `internal` (O2.1). Those clauses are only checked later, inside IssueSeq, after the room test. So in one state with the same static violation, grow returns `reject` when there is room and `fail` when there is not. The restricted issue (IssueR, issueR_fail_iff) and push both put the static clauses before `fail`. The grow docstring does not mention this. The report's (f) lists grow's pre-fail clauses (0 < extra, last block at the top, b releasable) and calls the choice 'par analogie avec §77', but MetaRight, a static clause of §77.1, is left out.\n186\t  EVIDENCE: Spec §77.4: \"Une région indisponible n'autorise pas à contourner un rejet statique préalable.\" §58: \"Pour alloc et issue, le contrôle des préconditions statiques précède la détermination de l'échec dynamique\". Counterexample, decided in scratch .../scratchpad/d1/review/q2grow/B.lean (axioms: propext, Quot.sound). opsZ := setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5, .kernel (.derive 2 5 .modify), .kernel (.enter [7] [])]: a callee frame that holds only a Modify on destination leaf 5 and has no right on stack value 0. Results: drun isSome = true; MetaRightM = false; ReleasePre 6 = true; PushStatic = false. Then grow 0 6 1 (room: 1+1 ≤ 3) gives \"reject\"; grow 0 6 5 (no room) gives \"fail\"; push 0 5 6 (no room) gives \"reject\". theorem ce_grow_fail_masks_static : ... ∧ (dexec K0 (final opsZ) (.grow 0 6 1)).isReject = true ∧ (dexec K0 (final opsZ) (.grow 0 6 5)).isFail = true ∧ (dexec K0 (final opsZ) (.push 0 5 6)).isReject = true := by decide\n187\t  VERDICT: I confirmed the behaviour. grow checks only `0 < extra`, `x.auth = b`, `x.base + x.size = st.top` and `ReleasePre` before its room test (DataStack.lean:198-199). MetaRightM is checked only later, inside IssueSeq/SplitPre (:200). In one reached state with no Modify on the stack value, the outcome therefore depends on the room. push checks MetaRightM before fail (:145, PushStatic :90-92), so the two operations are inconsistent, and grow does not follow the report's own rationale (f): 'Clauses statiques avant fail (§58), par analogie avec §77', since §77.1 includes MetaRight. I downgrade the severity from major to minor for three reasons. (1) The choice is reported: the grow docstring (:187-190) and report (f) list exactly the clauses checked before fail ('grow : 0 < extra, b dernier bloc et au sommet, b libérable'), so MetaRightM's absence is visible. (2) No normative text binds grow's order: §58 restricts the static-before-fail rule to alloc and issue 'dans ce noyau', and the brief says only 'fail si pas de place'. (3) What remains is a rationale that is applied inconsistently and not flagged, not an unreported semantic choice.\n188\t  VEVIDENCE: I reran a copy of q2grow/B.lean in verify-grow/B.lean. Output: drun isSome = true; (MetaRightM, ReleasePre 6, PushStatic) = (false, true, false); grow 0 6 1 = \"reject\"; grow 0 6 5 = \"fail\"; push 0 5 6 = \"reject\". 'ce_grow_fail_masks_static' depends on axioms: [propext, Quot.sound]. verify-grow/G.lean shows the reject of grow 0 6 1 comes from the split of internal, not from store: on releaseEff s 6 with region [2,3], (MetaRightM, SplitPre, StorePre, Active internal) = (false, false, true, true). Spec §77.4: \"Une région indisponible n'autorise pas à contourner un rejet statique préalable.\" Spec §58: \"seules alloc et issue peuvent l'émettre dans ce noyau ... Pour alloc et issue, le contrôle des préconditions statiques précède la détermination de l'échec dynamique\".\n189\t- Q2-DERIVED-NOT-RESTRICTED [major->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:87-108 (PushStatic, IssueSeq), :141-156 (push), :191-209 (grow); formal/reports/Derivability_v0.1.md (f) 'Décisions de représentation ... sans effet'\n190\t  CLAIM: The brief says push issues at the top through the restricted issue ('issue restreint'). push and grow instead use the derived sequence split+store (IssueSeq). This drops the §77.2 'non Init' clause of the restricted issue, so push accepts an already-Init destination that IssueR rejects, and grow in place works only because of that. This changes which programs are accepted. AGENTS.md calls such a choice a semantic question, but the report files it under 'Décisions de représentation prises ici (sans effet sur la question posée)'. The docstrings of push and PushStatic do not say that the non-Init clause is missing. The report does note, in (d) and (e) 2, that the brief's grow conflicts with §77.2.\n191\t  EVIDENCE: Spec §77.2: \"destination est une feuille utilisable, non Init\". Counterexamples, decided in scratch .../q2grow/E.lean and F.lean (axioms: propext, Quot.sound). (1) opsI := setup1 ++ [.kernel (.write 2 [5])]: I 5 = init; IssueStaticR on region [2] into 5 = false (it is true on final setup1, so only the Init clause separates them); dexec (.push 0 1 5) isOk = true. (2) theorem ce_grow_needs_init_dst: (dexec K0 (final opsA) (.grow 0 6 1)).isOk = true ∧ (releaseEff (final opsA).s 6).I 5 = InitSt.init ∧ IssueStaticR (releaseEff ..) st.arena [2,3,4] 5 = false, by decide.\n192\t  VERDICT: The counterexamples reproduce. push and grow use split+store (IssueSeq), which has no §77.2 non-Init clause, so push accepts an Init destination that IssueStaticR rejects, and grow in place relies on this. The claim that this is unreported is wrong, so major is not justified. Report (f) says 'DataStack.push et grow utilisent l'issue dérivé, sémantique de store. Ils acceptent une destination Init, ce que grow exige.' Report (d) adds: 'grow réémet dans une destination déjà Init ... IssueR le rejetterait (écart 2)'. The module docstring (DataStack.lean:12-13) says push is 'the derived issue ... split ... then store', and its line 23-24 points to report (f) for the decisions the brief leaves open. The brief also names 'the derived issue/reset' as the basis of Q2, and a grow into the same destination cannot be built on IssueR at all; the report raises this conflict in (e) 2. What remains is a categorisation error: this item sits under 'Décisions de représentation prises ici (sans effet sur la question posée, à confirmer)'. It changes accepted programs, so by AGENTS.md ('A choice that does is a semantic question') it is not a representation choice. Also, PushStatic and push do not say in their docstrings that the non-Init clause is absent.\n193\t  VEVIDENCE: I reran copies in verify-grow/E.lean and F.lean. E: drun opsI isSome = true; (IssueStaticR on [2] into 5, I 5 = init) = (false, true); without the write the same IssueStaticR = true; 'ce_push_init_dst' depends on axioms: [propext, Quot.sound]. F: 'ce_grow_needs_init_dst' depends on axioms: [propext, Quot.sound]. Spec §77.2: \"destination est une feuille utilisable, non Init\". Report line 198: \"### Décisions de représentation prises ici (sans effet sur la question posée, à confirmer)\". Report line 202 is the push/grow bullet quoted above.\n194\t- Q2-GROW-SAME-BASE-WORDING [minor->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:530-545 (grow_same_base docstring and statement); report (d) row grow_same_base\n195\t  CLAIM: The docstring says grow 'releases b' and gives a last block 'held by the same destination', and the report adds 'ancien bloc inactif'. The statement proves less. b is shown inactive only in the intermediate state `releaseEff d.s b`, not in the arrival state d'.s. 'Same destination' is shown only for the metadata field Blk.dst = x.dst, not for the kernel holder of the new authority. Both facts are true in the runs I checked, but neither is stated or proved.\n196\t  EVIDENCE: Statement line 536: `st'.blocks.getLast? = some ⟨x.base, x.size + extra, d.s.A.length, x.dst⟩` (metadata only). Line 539: `(releaseEff d.s b).A[b]?.map (·.active) = some false` (intermediate state, not d'.s). No conjunct mentions `d'.s.A[d.s.A.length]?` holder or `d'.s.A[b]?`. Eval of final opsG (scratch D.lean): auth 9 = (true, [2,3,4], some 5, \"P5\"), auth 6 = (false, ...). So the claims hold in this run but are not part of the theorem.\n197\t  VERDICT: Confirmed by reading the statement. The docstring says the new last block is 'held by the same destination'. The theorem shows only the metadata field (`st'.blocks.getLast? = some ⟨x.base, x.size + extra, d.s.A.length, x.dst⟩`), not the kernel holder of authority d.s.A.length in d'.s. §77 uses 'détenue par destination' for the kernel holder. The report's 'ancien bloc inactif' is proved only for the intermediate state `releaseEff d.s b`, not for d'.s. 'releases b' itself is accurately covered by the last conjunct. Both missing facts hold in case_grow, so this is a wording gap between the docstring/report and the statement: minor, as reported.\n198\t  VEVIDENCE: DataStack.lean:536 `st'.blocks.getLast? = some ⟨x.base, x.size + extra, d.s.A.length, x.dst⟩` and :539 `(releaseEff d.s b).A[b]?.map (·.active) = some false`. No conjunct mentions d'.s.A[b]? or the holder of d'.s.A[d.s.A.length]?. verify-grow/G.lean on final opsG gives (A[6].active, A[9].holder) = (some false, some \"P5\"), so the facts hold in that run but are not stated. Spec §77 effect: \"elle est détenue par destination\"."}]},"uuid":"566d5e74-cf0e-4075-93f4-dad7414ddba3","timestamp":"2026-11-03T14:34:54.592Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review.txt","content":"=== issue\nSUMMARY: Review of question 1, issue (formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean: restricted issue, the kernel sequence, issue_to_seq / seq_to_issue / issue_as_kernel_split / issue_as_kernel, ce_g2 / ce_init / ce_avail / ce_empty_region).\n\nThe equivalence theorems hold up. `issue_as_kernel_split`, `issue_as_kernel`, `issue_to_seq`, `seq_to_issue` and the four ce_* theorems depend only on propext and Quot.sound. Neither file contains sorry, admit, native_decide, axiom or noncomputable.\n\n- **Same arrival state.** It is really stated: `StoreStep … (.ok s')` uses the s' of `IssueR`, and `seq_to_issue` gives `IssueR … (.ok s₂ _)`. `issueEffR_eq` is a structural equality.\n- **Not vacuous.** On the reached state of opsArena, `IssueR arD [1] 3` is ok and `HasRight` on 3 holds in its arrival state (checked by decide). The hypotheses `hg2`, `hav` and `hni` are each needed: ce_g2, ce_avail and ce_init.\n- **No further deviation can exist.** The iff is machine-checked, so for reached states with `ar ∈ s.arenas` the only gaps between `IssueR` and split/derive then store are O8.5 (`HasRight` on the arrival state), availability and `Init`. The counterexamples the brief asks for (sequence accepts while `IssueR` rejects with the region available and the destination not `Init`, or `IssueR` accepts with the destination still exercisable while the sequence rejects) cannot exist.\n- **O8.3.** The owned-storage clause of store is discharged in reached states by `ownedIn_holds` through `RootLive`.\n- **Kernel versus restricted issue.** The differences are documented: `MetaRightM` (`metaRightM_iff`), destination outside the region (option 2, in the `IssueStaticR` and `issueStaticR_iff` docstrings) and the anchor added to `D dst` (`issueEffR` docstring). The extra `EffAnchorOK` conjunct of `ArrivalR` changes no outcome: `AnchorOrderHeld` on the arrival state already implies it, as the `seq_to_issue` proof uses.\n\nThe findings are about `IssueR` against §77 itself, not about the equivalence: one major and two minor, listed below.\n- Q1I-1 [major->major] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean:97-111 (IssueR, through kernel Avail at Ern/MM03.lean:7104 and the issueEff filter at :7080-7087); docstring of ce_empty_region at :1145-1149\n  CLAIM: `IssueR` reads availability leaf by leaf: a region counts as available when each of its leaves is in `ar.available`. The candidate text gives `available` as a set of regions and tests whether the region belongs to it. The text does not say which reading is meant, and the two readings give different outcomes. This interpretation is reported neither in the docstrings nor in the report. Under the leaf reading, the overlap clause of §77.5 can never be reached in a reached state, a region that partly overlaps an active emission gets `fail` where the region-set reading gives `reject`, and the empty region is always available.\n  EVIDENCE: Spec text. §46 types the field as `available: Set<Region>`; §77.4 says \"Si les conditions statiques tiennent mais que `region` n'appartient plus à `available`, l'opération produit `fail`\". §77.5 sits after the availability check and says \"Si la région est disponible, le mode effectif de `internal` … à défaut, l'issue est `reject`. La région ne peut chevaucher une région émise encore active.\" The effect says \"La région quitte `available`\".\n\nFormal side: kernel `Avail ar region := ∀ l ∈ region, l ∈ ar.available`. The `IssueR` docstring only says \"`fail` … when the region is not available (§77.4)\".\n\nCheck 1 (scratch b.lean, decide): reached state `run K0 [] (opsAlloc ++ [.issue 0 0 [5] 2])`. `reach_run` holds and `arenas = [⟨4, 4, [6], [([5], 5)]⟩]`. Block 5 is `([5], active, origin 4)`. The following typecheck: `IssueR arQ [5, 6] 3 s (.fail s arQ)` and `¬ IssueRight s arQ [5, 6]`. Under a region-set reading where issuing [5] removes only the region [5], [5,6] is still available and §77.5 gives `reject`. Under the leaf reading, ArenaInv clause 4 makes every region that overlaps an active emission unavailable, so the overlap sentence of §77.5 never applies.\n\nCheck 2 (scratch a.lean, decide): state `run K0 [] (opsArena ++ [.issue 0 0 [1] 2])`, whose arena is `⟨1, 4, [], [([1], 5)]⟩` (available empty). `IssueR arIss [] 3 s (.ok …)` typechecks and `Avail arIss [] ` holds, so the empty region is available although `available = []`. ce_empty_region states that this `ok` is acceptable \n  VERDICT: Confirmed. `IssueR` decides `fail` with the kernel's `Avail ar region := ∀ l ∈ region, l ∈ ar.available`. That is a leaf-by-leaf reading, possible only because the kernel `Arena.available` is a `List Nat` of leaves. The candidate text types the field as `Set<Region>` and tests whether the region belongs to it, so the text is open to more than one reading. §78 also says \"la totalité des régions du stockage administré redevient disponible\". The readings classify differently: on a reached state the leaf reading gives `fail` where a set-of-regions reading gives `reject` through §77.5. This choice is stated nowhere: not in the docstrings of `IssueR`/`IssueStaticR`/`ce_empty_region`, not in the kernel `Avail`/`Arena` docstrings, and not in the report's (c) gap list (whose item 7 lists other kernel-vs-candidate differences) or its (f) questions. Earlier reports (MM03 p2–p8) never arbitrate it either. Per AGENTS.md, a choice that changes behavior is a semantic question to report, and the brief explicitly asks for \"fail iff the region is unavailable in the metadata; reject otherwise\".\n  VEVIDENCE: Spec §46: \"available: Set<Region>\"; §77.4: \"que `region` n'appartient plus à `available`, l'opération produit fail\"; §77.5: \"La région ne peut chevaucher une région émise encore active.\"; §78 effect: \"la totalité des régions du stockage administré redevient disponible\". Kernel Ern/MM03.lean:7104 `def Avail (ar : Arena) (region : List Nat) : Prop := ∀ l ∈ region, l ∈ ar.available`. Scratch /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/verify-issue/v1.lean (compiles, no errors): `#eval (run K0 [] (opsAlloc ++ [.issue 0 0 [5] 2])).s.arenas` prints `[(4, 4, [6], [([5], 5)])]`. These hold: `Reachable K0 [] …` via reach_run, `IssueR arQ [5, 6] 3 s (.fail s arQ)` and `¬ IssueRight s arQ [5, 6]`. So the leaf reading gives fail where a set-of-regions reading (only [5] leaves `available`) reaches §77.5 and gives reject. Scratch v2.lean: with arIss = ⟨1,4,[],[([1],5)]⟩ (the reached entry after opsArena ++ [.issue 0 0 [1] 2]), `Avail arIss []` and `IssueR arIss [] 3 s (.ok …)` both typecheck, so the empty region counts as available even though `available = []`. A grep of the report and the Arena.lean docstrings for this reading finds nothing.\n- Q1I-2 [minor->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean:51-63 (IssueStaticR docstring and definition) and :97-111 (IssueR)\n  CLAIM: The first sentence of §77.1, \"L'entrée `arena` existe\", is not a clause of `IssueStaticR` or `IssueR`. Nothing writes the new metadata `ar'` back into the state either. Nothing ties `IssueR` to a registered entry: it accepts any `Arena` value, and its arrival state keeps the stale entry in `s.arenas`. The equivalence theorems are unaffected, since they all assume `har : ar ∈ s.arenas`. There is no restricted counterpart of the kernel's `IssueAt`.\n  EVIDENCE: Spec §77.1: \"L'entrée `arena` existe et est vivante ; `internal` est active.\" The `IssueStaticR` docstring reads clause 1 as \"restricted metadata right and `internal` active\" only.\n\nScratch a.lean, decide:\n- `arFake := ⟨1, 4, [1], [([7], 9)]⟩`. `arFake ∉ (run K0 [] opsArena).s.arenas` holds, yet `IssueR arFake [1] 3 (run K0 [] opsArena).s (.ok …)` typechecks.\n- On `run K0 [] opsAvail`, the registered entry fails (ce_avail), but the made-up value `⟨1, 4, [1], []⟩` gives `IssueR … (.ok …)`.\n- `#eval (issueEffR (run K0 [] opsArena).s arD [1] 3).1.arenas.map (fun a => (a.available, a.issued))` prints `[([1], [])]`: the arrival state still lists [1] as available and nothing as issued.\n  VERDICT: Confirmed. §77.1 begins \"L'entrée `arena` existe\". `IssueStaticR`'s docstring presents clause 1 as \"restricted metadata right and `internal` active\" and drops the existence requirement. `IssueR` takes any `Arena` value and yields `ok` for entries that are not in `s.arenas`. Every equivalence theorem assumes `har : ar ∈ s.arenas`, so no proven statement is affected. That keeps this minor: a definition and docstring coverage gap, not a wrong theorem. The sub-claim about write-back is weaker: `issueEffR` returns the new metadata as the second component, mirroring the kernel `IssueStep`, and the theorems equate it explicitly (`ar' = (issueEffR …).2`). Unlike the kernel's `IssueAt`, though, there is no restricted wrapper that looks up the entry and writes it back.\n  VEVIDENCE: Spec §77.1: \"L'entrée `arena` existe et est vivante ; `internal` est active.\" Arena.lean:51-56 docstring: \"clause 1, restricted metadata right and `internal` active\". Scratch /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/verify-issue/v2.lean compiles: `arFake := ⟨1, 4, [1], [([7], 9)]⟩`, `arFake ∉ (run K0 [] opsArena).s.arenas` by decide, and `IssueR arFake [1] 3 (run K0 [] opsArena).s (.ok …)` via `.ok (by decide) ×4`. Likewise `arMade := ⟨1,4,[1],[]⟩ ∉ (run K0 [] opsAvail).s.arenas`, but `IssueR arMade [1] 3 … (.ok …)`, while the registered entry fails (ce_avail). `#eval (issueEffR (run K0 [] opsArena).s arD [1] 3).1.arenas.map (fun a => (a.available, a.issued))` prints `[([1], [])]`.\n- Q1I-3 [minor->minor] real=False loc=formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean:51-56 (IssueStaticR docstring) and :57-63\n  CLAIM: The static part of §77.3 (\"restrictions statiques de validité et de non-échappement applicables à la destination\") has no counterpart in the formalization. The docstring says these restrictions \"are those of the kernel (`IssueStatic`, `issueStaticR_iff`)\", but `IssueStatic` has no conjunct for clause 3: its eight conjuncts encode §77.1 and §77.2 only. The text does not say what these restrictions are, and reading them as empty is an interpretation that is not flagged as one. This matters for classification: any such restriction is checked before `fail`, so leaving it empty can turn a static `reject` into `fail`. Under the kernel's `NoEscape`, which constrains only root anchors, the block (origin `internal`) is trivially non-escaping, so this may change no outcome. That is not stated or proven.\n  EVIDENCE: Spec §77.3: \"Les restrictions **statiques** de validité et de non-échappement applicables à la destination sont satisfaites. Le contrôle de `AnchorOrder` qui dépend de la nouvelle ancre est effectué sur **l'état d'arrivée simulé** …\".\n\nKernel `IssueStatic` (Ern/MM03.lean:7091-7097) is `MetaRight ∧ Active internal ∧ region ⊆ afp internal ∧ Usable dst ∧ HasRight dst ∧ dst not Init ∧ isLeaf dst ∧ dst holds no active authority`. Its docstring cites only \"MM-03.3 §6; A10 … J1\".\n\n`grep -n 'NoEscape' Ern/MM03.lean` finds it used in transfer (:1251, :1274) and depend (:1308), never in issue. The report does not list this reading among its questions.\n  VERDICT: Refuted. The docstring already documents the reading: \"The static restrictions of clause 3 are those of the kernel (`IssueStatic`, `issueStaticR_iff`); its `AnchorOrder` check is on the arrival state (`ArrivalR`).\" §77.3 never defines these restrictions, and the finding offers no outcome that changes. The only non-escape restriction the kernel has, `NoEscape`, constrains only anchors with no origin. The issued block always has origin `internal`, so `NoEscape` of the new block holds for every state, entry, region, destination and frame: adding it cannot turn any `fail` into `reject` or change any other outcome. The escape-related anchor checks of §77.3 and §77.6 are formalized in `ArrivalR` on the arrival state, as the text requires. The finding itself concedes \"this may change no outcome\". With no counterexample and a documented reading, there is no finding.\n  VEVIDENCE: Arena.lean:55-56: \"The static restrictions of clause 3 are those of the kernel (`IssueStatic`, `issueStaticR_iff`); its `AnchorOrder` check is on the arrival state (`ArrivalR`).\" Kernel Ern/MM03.lean:723: `def NoEscape (s : S3) (x : Nat) (fd : Option Nat) : Prop := OptP (s.A[x]?) (fun y => y.origin = none → …) True`. Scratch /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/verify-issue/v2.lean proves, with no error: `theorem noEscape_block (s : S3) (ar : Arena) (region : List Nat) (dst : Nat) (fd : Option Nat) : NoEscape (issueEffR s ar region dst).1 s.A.length fd := by simp [NoEscape, issueEffR, issueEff, S3.addAuth, OptP]`. A kernel non-escape check on the destination is therefore vacuous for issue.\n=== reset-newarena\nSUMMARY: Verdict: changes (two minor findings, no blocking or major). All checks were by Lean runs against the built modules. Scratch files are in /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/reset/ (T1–T5.lean). No repository file was modified.\n\n(1) ResetPreR against §78, clause by clause. MetaRightM is the first branch of §78.1. Clause 2 (`internal ∈ ancs j → ¬Active j`) is §78.2. Clause 3 reads §78.3 with `internal` included. That reading is not silent: the ResetPreR and reset_clause3_strict docstrings and the report (f)1 all name it, and the report flags the ambiguity for arbitration. The consequence is correctly shown, by ce_reset_anchor and reset_blocked_by_stored_internal. On the opsArena state, MetaRightM, §78.2 and the strict form of clause 3 all hold, so only the inclusive part (`4 ∈ D 0`) blocks the reset. After `transfer 0 2`, the anchor follows to D 2 and the reset stays blocked. The one deviation is that ResetPreR omits §78.1's liveness and existence (F1).\n\n(2) The reset equivalence is proven in both directions and is not vacuous: reset_alloc_ok holds on a reached live arena. hemp and hanc are exactly the gaps:\n- ce_reset_empty: MetaRightM and clause 3 hold, so only hemp fails.\n- ce_reset_anchor: clause 2 holds, so only hanc fails.\n\nThe brief expected clause 3 to follow from release's preconditions. The file says it does not, and that is correct: ReleasePre (MM03.lean:1004) only checks anchors equal to the released authority itself, which is fresh. ce_reset_anchor has ReleaseStep ok while clause 3 fails. hact is an extra hypothesis that comes from F1, not from the spec. Only one reading of §78.3 is covered (F2).\n\n(3) ce_writeShared matches the brief. It has:\n- a reached state (a6_reachable);\n- `issued = []`;\n- the kernel issue and reset ok;\n- ¬MetaRightM;\n- derive and split of internal on the region, and derive on the storage, all rejected;\n- IssueR rejected and ¬ResetPreR.\n\nTwo required items are not conjuncts of the theorem, but I checked they hold. The Observe access: a6.A[4] is Observe on [0], origin 0, and a6.top = 2. The SharedMutable metadata: metaLeaves a6 ar7 = [0] and KA marks leaf 0. The issue under the candidate text with the full MetaRight is also ok in a6 (IssueStatic, 6 ∉ [4], Avail, IssueRight, ArrivalR), and so is ResetPre KA a6 ar7 4.\n\n(4) newArena_as_library:\n- ReachableNG has init, every Step (all Reachable constructors, by reachableNG_of_reachable) and the unguarded newArenaNG.\n- newArenaPre_iff shows NewArenaPreNG is exactly the kernel precondition minus the guard.\n- Inv57 plus the conclusion covers the whole §57 conjunction: WF3, Inv3 (I1–I4, AnchorOrder, NoDangling), AnchorOrderHeld, NoOrphan via RootLive, and ArenaInv of every live entry.\n- step_inv57 and newArenaNG_inv57 compile from Inv57 alone, without reachability.\n- ce_two_admins is a non-vacuous ReachableNG state: two live entries share internal 4, and the double issue is rejected through the effective right.\n\nI found no evidence that the \"no safety property depends on the guard\" conclusion is overstated:\n- The kernel has no theorem that uses the guard clause.\n- The DataStack proofs do not use Guard56 or KGuard.\n- HandlePool's PReach already allows a pool and an arena sharing internal 4, and two pools sharing internal 4 (T2: `([4],[4])` and `([4,4],[])`), and its pool theorems are proven over PReach.\n\nThe theorem's own docstring correctly limits the claim to §57. The §56 uniqueness property fails trivially without the guard, which ce_two_admins shows.\n\nAxioms: all the theorems in focus depend only on propext and Quot.sound. The sorry/admit/native_decide/axiom/noncomputable grep on Arena.lean is empty.\n\nNot checked: the issue-side theorems and DataStack, which were outside this review's focus.\n- F1 [minor->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean:659-667 (ResetPreR), used by reset_to_seq/seq_to_reset/reset_as_kernel (714-803)\n  CLAIM: ResetPreR leaves out the first sentence of §78.1, \"L'entrée existe et est vivante.\" It is therefore more permissive than §78. It is also inconsistent with IssueStaticR, which does ask `s.Active ar.internal` (line 58). reset_as_kernel makes up for this with the section hypotheses `har` and `hact`. The docstrings name only hemp/hanc as the gaps of the equivalence. `hact` is not a spec gap: it is needed only because the definition drops liveness. With liveness in the definition, both sides would imply it (DerivePre asks `s.Active src`). The ResetPreR docstring presents the definition as the §78 preconditions and does not mention the omission. No stated theorem is false, because every consumer carries hact.\n  EVIDENCE: Scratch run (/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/reset/T1.lean), all by decide on opsDead := opsArena ++ [.release 4]: `Reachable K0 [] (run K0 [] opsDead).s` (reach_run), `arenas = [arD]`, `¬ Active 4`, `ResetPreR (run K0 [] opsDead).s arD` HOLDS, while `ResetAtStep K0 0 0 _ .reject`, `DeriveStep 4 1 .modify _ .reject` and `IssueR arD [1] 2 _ .reject`. So on a reached state with a dead registered entry, ResetPreR accepts. The kernel reset, the derive+release sequence and the restricted issue all reject. Spec §78.1: \"L'entrée existe et est vivante.\"\n  VERDICT: Reproduced. ResetPreR (Arena.lean:659-667) has only three conjuncts: MetaRightM, §78.2, and inclusive §78.3. It drops the first sentence of §78.1, \"L'entrée existe et est vivante.\" Its docstring presents it as the \"Preconditions of the restricted `reset` (candidate Spec 1 §78...)\" and cites §78.1 only for the metadata right. No docstring says the definition leaves liveness out. The theorem docstrings do limit their domain (\"for a live arena\" on reset_to_seq and reset_as_kernel). Every positive use of ResetPreR carries the hypotheses har/hact or sits on a live arena (reset_alloc_ok), so no stated theorem is false. That makes this a wording/citation issue, not a blocking one. Mitigation: the omission copies the kernel, where ResetPre has no liveness clause and ResetAtPre adds it, while IssueStatic does include Active. It is still a definition more permissive than §78, and its docstring does not say so.\n  VEVIDENCE: I ran /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/verify-reset/V1.lean through lake env lean. Every example passed with no error; a sanity check in V0.lean shows a false decide does report an error. With opsDead := opsArena ++ [.release 4], the following all hold: `Reachable K0 [] (run K0 [] opsDead).s`, `arenas = [arD]`, `¬ Active 4`, `ResetPreR (run K0 [] opsDead).s arD`, `ResetAtStep K0 0 0 _ .reject`, `DeriveStep 4 1 .modify _ .reject`, `IssueR arD [1] 2 _ .reject`. Spec §78.1: \"L'entrée existe et est vivante.\" ResetPreR docstring: \"1 restricted metadata right (§78.1)\", with no mention of liveness.\n- F2 [minor->minor] real=False loc=formal/Ern/Derived/Arena.lean:721-731 (reset_to_seq), 805-813 (reset_clause3_strict)\n  CLAIM: The §78.3 ambiguity (\"sous-arbre d'origines de internal\", inclusive or strict) is reported as open (ResetPreR docstring; report (f)1). The brief's equivalence, however, is stated only under the inclusive reading. reset_to_seq takes the inclusive clause 3 as a hypothesis, but its proof discards that clause (`obtain ⟨hm, h2, -⟩ := h`). As a result, neither direction of the strict-reading equivalence (strict ResetPre ↔ derive;release ok ∧ hemp) can be obtained from the stated theorems. reset_clause3_strict only gives strict clause 3 from clause 2. If arbitration picks reading (A) of report (e)4, no reset_as_kernel theorem covers it.\n  EVIDENCE: Line 727 of reset_to_seq: `obtain ⟨hm, h2, -⟩ := h` (clause 3 unused). Scratch T2.lean, by decide on the reached state run K0 [] opsArena: `ResetPreStrict s arD ∧ ¬ ResetPreR s arD`, where ResetPreStrict = MetaRightM ∧ §78.2 ∧ (∀ usable q, ∀ x ∈ D q, internal ∉ ancs x). On this state reset_to_seq cannot be applied, although its conclusion holds (ce_reset_anchor: DeriveStep 4 1 ok, ReleaseStep ok).\n  VERDICT: The facts are right: reset_to_seq discards clause 3 at line 727, and the opsArena state satisfies the strict reading but not ResetPreR. They do not make a finding, for three reasons. (1) The reading is not chosen silently. The ResetPreR docstring says \"internal itself included, as the kernel reads §78.3\". reset_clause3_strict's docstring states the strict reading. ce_reset_anchor documents this same state: rejected by clause 3 under the kernel reading, while derive then release are ok. AGENTS asks that an ambiguity be reported, and it was. (2) The brief asks for one reset_as_kernel equivalence. It does not ask for one per reading of an ambiguous passage. Asking for a strict-reading equivalence, in case arbitration later picks reading (A), is an addition beyond the task. (3) reset_to_seq's statement matches the brief (restricted reset implies the sequence is ok). A hypothesis the proof does not use makes the theorem less general than it could be. That does not make it vacuous or weaker than claimed.\n  VEVIDENCE: I confirmed `sed -n 725,728p`: line 727 is `obtain ⟨hm, h2, -⟩ := h`. V2.lean (`ResetPreStrict (run K0 [] opsArena).s arD ∧ ¬ ResetPreR (run K0 [] opsArena).s arD` by decide) compiles. This is the ce_reset_anchor state, already documented. ResetPreR docstring (Arena.lean:659-663): \"3 no usable place with an anchor in the subtree of origins of `internal`, `internal` itself included, as the kernel reads §78.3\". reset_clause3_strict docstring: \"Under the strict reading of §78.3, the clause follows from §78.2 and I3 in reached states.\"\n=== datastack-aba\nSUMMARY: I reviewed formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean against the brief (Q2) and Spec 1 candidate §56, §58, §62, §65, §77 and §78, read-only. Each claim was checked with #eval/decide runs from DS.init under K0. Scratch files: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/ds2/t1.lean to t6.lean. #print axioms for mark_no_aba, rewind_rejects_live_above, rewind_keeps_older_marks and case_aba lists only propext and Quot.sound.\n\n(1) Choices the brief leaves open, and whether a docstring states them:\n- Static checks before fail: stated for push (PushStatic) and grow, but PushStatic omits dst ∉ region (DS-5).\n- 0 < extra: stated.\n- grow's destination: stated vaguely as 'the destination of the block'; it is the recorded dst, not the current holder (DS-8).\n- `above` region: stated, with a false justification (DS-2).\n- 'covers a region issued after m' read as 'covers a cell in [m.top, st.top)': stated in the theorem docstring, but the claimed coincidence is false (DS-2).\n- mark requires no right: implicit; a nonexistent stack gives reject for mark and rewind, not stated in docstrings (DS-6).\n- Blocks kept by the filter: docstring inaccurate (DS-7).\n- KGuard on kernel newArena: stated.\n- push/grow use the store-based derived issue instead of the restricted issue: not stated in docstrings, and the report wrongly calls it representation-only (DS-4).\n- push of size 0: not stated (DS-3).\n\n(2) mark_no_aba is sound, and I found no DOp sequence that makes the rewound mark valid again. Fresh ids are global, the stack index is part of the mark, and stacks are never removed. MarksWF is satisfiable and is established for library runs by drun_init_marksWF. However, the statement covers only the mark passed to rewind, not the later marks removed with it, and mark uniqueness is never proved (DS-1, blocking).\n\n(3) `above` does not capture 'regions issued after m'. After a grow that straddles a mark, or after an intermediate rewind to a later mark, an active authority derived from internal can cover part of a post-m region and rewind m is still ok (DS-2). An empty-footprint block also passes (DS-3). In the other direction, rewind rejects for missing rights with nothing derived above, and the theorem called 'converse' is only the contrapositive (DS-6).\n\n(4) rewind_keeps_older_marks proves what it states: marks = pre, top = m.top, other stacks unchanged, I unchanged. That 'later marks are removed' depends on DS-1's missing uniqueness.\n\nNot checked: grow_keeps_prefix_bytes in depth, and the Q1 module beyond the definitions DataStack uses.\n- DS-1 [blocking->blocking] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:690-711 (mark_no_aba); also 503-517 (rewind_keeps_older_marks), 576-577 (MarksWF)\n  CLAIM: mark_no_aba is weaker than the brief and than its own docstring. It concludes only about the mark passed to rewind. A rewind also removes every later mark of the stack, and no theorem says those marks never become valid again. The brief asks this for any removed mark: 'une marque retirée ne redevient jamais valide'. Nothing establishes that marks are unique either. MarksWF only states m.stack = i and m.id < fresh, so 'later marks removed' in rewind_keeps_older_marks (st'.marks = pre) holds only under an unproven pre/post disjointness.\n  EVIDENCE: Statement (#check): `MarksWF d → rewind k d j m = Out.ok d₁ → drun k d₁ os = some d' → (∀ i st, d'.stacks[i]? = some st → ¬m ∈ st.marks) ∧ ∀ st, d'.stacks[j]? = some st → rewind k d' j m = Out.fail`, which is about `m` only. Docstring: 'a mark removed by `rewind` is live in no stack in any later state'. Run e6 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .push 0 1 5, .mark 0, .kernel (.release 6), .rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩] is ok, and its final stacks are [(0, [], [])]. So ⟨0,1,1⟩ is removed by rewinding ⟨0,0,0⟩, and mark_no_aba says nothing about it. `grep -n Gone` shows Gone is established only for the rewound mark (line 698).\n  VERDICT: Confirmed. mark_no_aba covers only the mark passed to rewind. The brief says 'une marque retirée ne redevient jamais valide', and the brief's own rewind rule says that m and all marks above it are removed, so the later marks are removed marks as well. No theorem covers them. Gone is established only for m (line 698), and no uniqueness invariant on marks exists (grep finds no Nodup or uniqueness lemma). MarksWF does not imply uniqueness: a MarksWF state with a duplicated later mark keeps that mark live after rewind. The theorem is therefore weaker than its own docstring and than report (d), which both say 'a mark removed by rewind is live in no stack'. The docstring of rewind_keeps_older_marks says 'm and the later marks are removed', and that also needs the same unproven uniqueness. In reachable states the property probably holds, but nothing proves it.\n  VEVIDENCE: #check @mark_no_aba gives a conclusion about `m` only: `(∀ i st, d'.stacks[i]? = some st → ¬m ∈ st.marks) ∧ ∀ st, d'.stacks[j]? = some st → rewind k d' j m = Out.fail`. Reproduced E6 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .push 0 1 5, .mark 0, .kernel (.release 6), .rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩]: ok, stacks [(0, [], [])], so ⟨0,1,1⟩ is removed without being the rewound mark. Scratch v2/v3: dDup = final setup1 with stack-0 marks [⟨0,1,0⟩, ⟨0,0,0⟩, ⟨0,1,0⟩] and fresh 2. The MarksWF check by List.all gives `true`. `rewind K0 dDup 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩` gives (\"ok\", [(0, [], [{stack := 0, id := 1, top := 0}])]): the later mark ⟨0,1,0⟩ is still live. So the hypotheses of mark_no_aba cannot yield the claim for later marks.\n- DS-2 [major->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:74-76 (above), 167-185 (rewind), 481-501 (rewind_rejects_live_above, rewind_ok_no_live_above)\n  CLAIM: `above st m` = cells [m.top, st.top) does not capture 'regions issued after m'. The docstrings of `above` and rewind_rejects_live_above say 'the regions issued after m lie there', and two reached runs show this is false. In each run, an active authority derived from internal covers part of a region issued after m, yet rewind m is ok. The report (f) 5 describes the reading as 'couvre une cellule au-dessus du sommet de m'. The code is narrower than that: it also stops at the current top (run E8).\n  EVIDENCE: E4 = setup1 ++ [.push 0 2 5, .mark 0, .grow 0 6 1, .kernel (.release 7), .kernel (.derive 5 2 .modify)]. drun ok; stacks [(3,[⟨0,3,7,5⟩],[⟨0,0,2⟩])]; afp 7 = [2,3,4], the region issued after the mark. A[8] = (active true, origin 5, fp [2]), ancs 8 = [5,1]. dexec rewind 0 ⟨0,0,2⟩ = \"ok\". E8 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .push 0 1 5, .mark 0, .push 0 1 6, .kernel (.release 7), .rewind 0 ⟨0,1,1⟩, .kernel (.release 6), .kernel (.derive 5 3 .modify)]. Cell 3 is block 7's region, issued after ⟨0,0,0⟩. A[9] = (true, some 5, [3]), ancs [5,1]. rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩ = \"ok\", and the next push 0 2 7 = \"reject\". Brief: 'reject si une autorité active dérivée de internal couvre une région émise après m'.\n  VERDICT: The runs reproduce, and the docstring justification is false. The docstrings of `above` and rewind_rejects_live_above say 'the regions issued after m lie there'. E4 contradicts this: the grown region [2,3,4] (block ⟨0,3,7,5⟩, issued by grow after mark ⟨0,0,2⟩) does not lie in above = [4]. The report (f) 5 describes the implemented reading as 'couvre une cellule au-dessus du sommet de m'. E8 contradicts that too: the code takes only [m.top, current top), and authority 9 covers cell 3, which is above m's top, yet rewind is ok. I downgrade to minor. The interpretation question is reported for arbitration in (f) 5. The definition of `above` and its docstring ('those that rewind m returns') state the real bound. Both behaviors are safety-neutral: in E4 the covered cell 2 is not returned by the rewind, and in E8 cell 3 was already available above the top. What is wrong is wording and the report's description, not an unreported deviation.\n  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1. E4: drun ok, stacks [(3, [⟨0,3,7,5⟩], [⟨0,0,2⟩])], A[8] = (true, some 5, [2]), ancs 8 = [5,1], A[7] = (false, some 5, [2,3,4]); `rewind 0 ⟨0,0,2⟩` = \"ok\", and after it stacks = [(2, [⟨0,3,7,5⟩], [])]. E8: drun ok, stacks [(1, [⟨0,1,6,5⟩], [⟨0,0,0⟩])], A[9] = (true, some 5, [3]), ancs [5,1]; `rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩` = \"ok\"; then push 0 2 7 = \"reject\" and push 0 1 7 = \"ok\". Report (f) 5: '« Couvre une région émise après m » est lu comme « couvre une cellule au-dessus du sommet de m »'.\n- DS-3 [major->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:141-156 (push), 171-185 (rewind)\n  CLAIM: push accepts size 0, and neither a docstring nor the report states this choice. The size-0 block is an active authority derived from internal with an empty footprint. rewind of an earlier mark is then ok while that authority stays active, and rewind drops it from `blocks`. This is the empty-footprint gap that Q1 reports for reset (ce_reset_empty, (e) 5). DataStack's rewind inherits it, and neither the DataStack docstrings nor report (d)/(f) mention it.\n  EVIDENCE: E3 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .push 0 0 5]: drun ok. dexec rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩ = \"ok\". After the rewind, stacks = [(0, [], [])] while A[6] = some (true, some 5, []) and ancs 6 = [5,1]: an active derived block that the metadata no longer tracks. Brief: reject when 'une autorité active dérivée de internal couvre une région émise après m'. The region issued after m is [], which authority 6 covers.\n  VERDICT: The run reproduces, but part of the claim is wrong. Accepting push of size 0 is not an undocumented choice. Push is the restricted issue, and the restricted issue accepts the empty region (ce_empty_region: 'the restricted issue of the empty region is ok'). The rewind verdict follows the rule stated in rewind's docstring ('rejected when an active authority derived from internal covers one of them', meaning the cells above m) and the reading reported in (f) 5. What remains confirmed and undocumented is a consequence. An active, empty-footprint block issued after m does not block rewind and is dropped from `blocks` while still active. A reading in which the block's own authority covers its issued region would reject this, as the brief's literal text would. Neither the DataStack docstrings nor (d)/(f) tie this to the empty-footprint gap that Q1 reports for reset ((c) 4, (e) 5, (f) 3). This is minor: the gap is a consequence of a documented reading, and an empty footprint has no safety effect.\n  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1. E3 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .push 0 0 5]: drun ok, stacks [(0, [⟨0,0,6,5⟩], [⟨0,0,0⟩])]; `rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩` = \"ok\". After the rewind, stacks = [(0, [], [])], A[6] = some (true, some 5, []), ancs 6 = [5,1]. Arena.lean:1146-1153 ce_empty_region: IssueR of [] is ok, so push 0 matches the restricted issue.\n- DS-4 [major->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:97-108 (IssueSeq), 139-156 (push), 187-209 (grow); module docstring 10-18\n  CLAIM: The brief asks push to issue at the top through the restricted issue. push and grow instead use the kernel sequence split then store, which Q1 proved differs from IssueR (ce_init, ce_g2). As a result push accepts an Init destination, which §77.2 forbids. No docstring says that push or grow depart from the restricted issue. Report (f) lists this under 'Décisions de représentation … sans effet sur la question posée', but it changes accepted programs.\n  EVIDENCE: E1 = setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5, .kernel (.release 6)]: drun ok and (final e1).s.I 5 == .init is true. dexec push 0 1 5 = \"ok\". §77.2: 'destination est une feuille utilisable, non Init'. AGENTS.md: 'A choice that does [change an accepted program] is a semantic question: stop and report.'\n  VERDICT: Confirmed that push and grow use the kernel sequence split+store and therefore accept an Init destination, which §77.2 forbids for the restricted issue. The deviation and its effect are documented, though. The module docstring and the IssueSeq docstring state that push and grow are split then store. Report (f) says 'Ils acceptent une destination Init, ce que grow exige'. Report (d) says 'IssueR le rejetterait (écart 2)'. The brief also asks for Q2 to be built 'on kernel ops and the derived issue'. What is left is a mislabel: the report files the choice under 'Décisions de représentation … sans effet sur la question posée', although it changes accepted programs (AGENTS.md: such a choice is a semantic question). Because the effect itself is reported, this is minor, not major.\n  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1. E1 = setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5, .kernel (.release 6)]: drun ok, (final e1).s.I 5 == .init = true, and `push 0 1 5` = \"ok\". §77.2: « destination est une feuille utilisable, non Init ». Report (f): « DataStack.push et grow utilisent l'issue dérivé, sémantique de store. Ils acceptent une destination Init, ce que grow exige. »\n- DS-5 [minor->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:87-92 (PushStatic), 145-155\n  CLAIM: PushStatic says it gathers the static preconditions checked before the room, but it omits §77.2 'destination not in region', which the spec orders before the availability fail. A destination inside the requested top region therefore gives fail when room is short, and reject when room is enough.\n  EVIDENCE: In final setup1, PushStatic s st 2 holds and 2 ∈ cellsOf st 0 4 (decide: true; cells [2,3,4]). dexec push 0 4 2 = \"fail\", while dexec push 0 3 2 = \"reject\". §77.2: 'la feuille destination n'appartient pas à region … Cette condition est statique et est vérifiée avant le fail de disponibilité.'\n  VERDICT: Confirmed. PushStatic and the static clause list in report (f) ('par analogie avec §77') omit the §77.2 static clause that the destination is outside the region. That clause must be checked before the availability fail. In push, the check arrives only through store's O8.3, which runs after the room check. A destination inside the requested top region therefore gives fail when room is short and reject when room is enough. The restricted issue would reject in both cases. Neither the docstring nor the report mentions this. It follows from the documented choice of the sequence (DS-4) and changes only a fail/reject classification, so minor.\n  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1: on final setup1, PushStatic s st 2 = true and cellsOf st 0 4 = [2,3,4]; push 0 4 2 = \"fail\", push 0 3 2 = \"reject\". Scratch v2 variant: after setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5], push 0 3 3 = \"fail\" and push 0 2 3 = \"reject\". §77.2: « la feuille destination n'appartient pas à region … Cette condition est statique et est vérifiée avant le fail de disponibilité. »\n- DS-6 [minor->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:167-173 (rewind docstring and none branch), 493-501 (rewind_ok_no_live_above)\n  CLAIM: The rewind docstring gives one reject reason, a covering derived authority, and says fail for a mark that is not live. The code also rejects (a) when the current frame cannot exercise internal at Modify, with nothing derived from internal active, and (b) for a nonexistent stack, where the brief says fail ('fail si m n'est pas une marque vivante de cette pile'). rewind_ok_no_live_above is called the 'Converse' but is the contrapositive of rewind_rejects_live_above. The actual converse is false (run E5).\n  EVIDENCE: E5 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .kernel (.derive 0 0 .observe)]: the active authorities and their ancs are 0..4:[], 5:[1], 6:[0], so nothing descends from 5. above = []. dexec rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩ = \"reject\", while mark 0 = \"ok\". dexec (final setup1) (.rewind 3 ⟨3,0,0⟩) = \"reject\".\n  VERDICT: Partly confirmed. The docstring of rewind_ok_no_live_above and report (d) ('réciproque') present it as the converse of rewind_rejects_live_above. It is the contrapositive restricted to ok, and the real converse is false. In E5 the mark is live and no authority derived from internal covers any cell above it (above = []), yet rewind rejects, because the frame can no longer exercise internal at Modify (O2.1 of the split). The rewind docstring names only the covering-authority reject. The nonexistent-stack reject is documented in report (f) ('une pile inexistante donne reject'), so that part is not a finding. Wording only, so minor.\n  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1. E5 = setup1 ++ [.mark 0, .kernel (.derive 0 0 .observe)]: drun ok; the active authorities with their ancs are 0-4 [], 5 (origin 1) [1], 6 (origin 0, Observe on [0]) [0]; above st ⟨0,0,0⟩ = []; `rewind 0 ⟨0,0,0⟩` = \"reject\", while `mark 0` = \"ok\". Also `rewind 3 ⟨3,0,0⟩` on final setup1 = \"reject\".\n- DS-7 [minor->minor] real=False loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:167-170, 182\n  CLAIM: The rewind docstring says 'the blocks above m are forgotten'. The filter (·.base < m.top) keeps a grown block that straddles m, so after the rewind the metadata holds a block extending above the new top.\n  EVIDENCE: E4 followed by rewind 0 ⟨0,0,2⟩ gives stacks [(2, [⟨0,3,7,5⟩], [])]: top 2, and a kept block of base 0 and size 3 covering cell index 2 above m.\n  VERDICT: The run reproduces, but the docstring is not contradicted and the rule is documented. A grown block whose base lies below m.top straddles m; it is not 'above m', so keeping it does not contradict 'the blocks above m are forgotten'. Report (f) states the filter explicitly: 'les blocs conservés sont ceux dont la base est sous le sommet de m'. Report (f) 5 notes that a grown block can straddle the top of m. A kept straddling block is also necessarily inactive. Its authority descends from internal and covers a cell in [m.top, top), so an active one makes rewind reject (rewind_rejects_live_above). The stale size has no safety effect.\n  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1: E4b gives stacks [(2, [⟨0,3,7,5⟩], [])] with A[7] = (false, some 5, [2,3,4]), so the kept block is inactive. DataStack.lean:182 `blocks := st.blocks.filter (·.base < m.top)`. Report (f): « les blocs conservés sont ceux dont la base est sous le sommet de m ».\n- DS-8 [minor->minor] real=False loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:187-206 (grow)\n  CLAIM: grow stores the grown block into the destination recorded at push (x.dst), not into the place that now holds b. After a kernel transfer of the block value, grow releases b from its new holder and stores the new block into the old, Moved place. The docstring phrase 'the destination of the block' does not make this clear, and report (f) does not list grow's destination among the decisions.\n  EVIDENCE: E7 = setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5, .kernel (.transfer 5 6)]: A[6] is active and held by place 6, while blocks record dst 5. After .grow 0 6 1, the run is ok, A[7] (fp [2,3]) is held by place 5, and A[6] is inactive and held by 6.\n  VERDICT: The run reproduces, but the behavior is documented and the brief does not fix the destination of grow. The grow docstring says 'into the destination of the block', where Blk.dst is the destination recorded at issue. grow_same_base says the new block is 'held by the same destination' and its statement names x.dst. Report (d) says 'vers la même destination'. Storing into a Moved leaf is allowed by §77.2 ('elle peut notamment être Uninit ou Moved'), and the brief's 'in place only' concerns the region (same base), not the holder.\n  VEVIDENCE: Scratch v1. E7 = setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5, .kernel (.transfer 5 6)]: ok, A[6] active and held by place 6, blocks dst 5. After .grow 0 6 1: ok, stacks [(2, [⟨0,2,7,5⟩], [])], A[7] = (true, held by 5, [2,3]), A[6] = (false, held by 6). DataStack.lean:187-190 docstring: « the derived issue of the region of the same base and of size size + extra into the destination of the block ».\n=== datastack-grow-cases\nSUMMARY: Three findings in question 2: two major, one minor. Everything else I was asked to check passed.\n\nDone criteria, run myself:\n- `lake build` succeeds (\"Build completed successfully (12 jobs)\"). Compiling Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean and Ern/Derived/Arena.lean directly with `lake env lean` gives no warnings.\n- The sorry/admit/native_decide/axiom grep prints nothing, and there is no `noncomputable` and no `set_option`.\n- `#print axioms` gives only [propext, Quot.sound] for the main theorems of both modules (dexec_reach, drun_reach, rewind_*, mark_no_aba, grow_*, all case_* and cases_reachable, issue_as_kernel(_split), reset_as_kernel, newArena_as_library).\n- MM03, MM23, HandlePool and Horrors are unchanged since 7d24495.\n\nFindings:\n- **grow ordering (major).** grow returns `fail` for lack of room before it checks the static clauses of the issue it performs (MetaRightM, §77.1). In one reached state with no right on the stack value, grow gives `reject` with room and `fail` without, while push gives `reject` (§77.4, §58).\n- **Derived issue instead of restricted issue (major).** push and grow use split+store instead of the restricted issue named in the brief. push therefore accepts an Init destination that §77.2 and IssueR reject, and grow in place depends on this. The report calls this a representation decision \"sans effet\", although it changes which programs are accepted.\n- **grow_same_base wording (minor).** Its docstring says more than the statement proves: b is shown inactive only in the intermediate state, and \"same destination\" is shown only in the metadata.\n\nWhat checked out:\n1. **The grow theorems.**\n   - grow_keeps_prefix_bytes reads the MM-02 trace correctly: release and split map to [] in MM23.assoc and are NoStep, and store maps to storeOps π.pe s2 π.L s3 a p with s3 the pre-store state (growMid).\n   - LayoutWF depends only on P, which release, split and store leave unchanged, so taking it on d.s is equivalent. Taking it with an arbitrary s2 rather than Repr makes the theorem more general.\n   - rangeBytes returns AByte values that carry provenance, so \"bytes and provenance\" is covered.\n   - The proof rests on the right reason: dst is not in the new region (O8.3, from StorePre) plus LayoutWF.disjoint.\n   - It is not vacuous. In scratch C.lean I applied the theorem to the case_grow state with an explicit layout and MM-02 state (LayoutWF decided). Prefix cells 2 and 3 are unchanged and destination 5 is rewritten (45 becomes 7).\n   - grow_keeps_prefix_init and grow_same_base are non-vacuous (case_grow).\n2. **Reachability.** dexec_reach and drun_reach are correct: push is split+store, rewind is split+release, grow is release+split+store, and kernel steps go through kexec_step / reachable_step. kexec covers only kernel ops.\n3. **Cases.** Every case states that its run succeeded (isSome) and is decided.\n   - The case_rewind rejection comes from SplitPre being false on above = [4].\n   - case_frame is a real mark-at-entry / rewind-at-exit pattern: the mark is taken right after enter, endUse 8 deactivates block 8, rewind runs in frame 2, and exit returns to frame 1 with an empty stack.\n   - case_aba, case_grow and case_stacks show what their docstrings say. The stack-1 rewind in case_stacks is over an empty region, which matches its docstring.\n\nAll scratch files are under /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/d1/review/q2grow/ (A.lean axioms, B.lean grow ordering, C.lean non-vacuity, D.lean case inspection, E.lean/F.lean Init destination).\n\nNot checked: rewind_rejects_live_above, mark_no_aba, rewind_keeps_older_marks and Q1, beyond running their axioms.\n- Q2-GROW-ORDER [major->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:191-209 (grow), compared with push :141-156 and PushStatic :87-92\n  CLAIM: grow decides `fail` (no room) before it checks the static clauses of the issue it performs: MetaRightM (§77.1) and that the current frame can exercise `internal` (O2.1). Those clauses are only checked later, inside IssueSeq, after the room test. So in one state with the same static violation, grow returns `reject` when there is room and `fail` when there is not. The restricted issue (IssueR, issueR_fail_iff) and push both put the static clauses before `fail`. The grow docstring does not mention this. The report's (f) lists grow's pre-fail clauses (0 < extra, last block at the top, b releasable) and calls the choice 'par analogie avec §77', but MetaRight, a static clause of §77.1, is left out.\n  EVIDENCE: Spec §77.4: \"Une région indisponible n'autorise pas à contourner un rejet statique préalable.\" §58: \"Pour alloc et issue, le contrôle des préconditions statiques précède la détermination de l'échec dynamique\". Counterexample, decided in scratch .../scratchpad/d1/review/q2grow/B.lean (axioms: propext, Quot.sound). opsZ := setup1 ++ [.push 0 1 5, .kernel (.derive 2 5 .modify), .kernel (.enter [7] [])]: a callee frame that holds only a Modify on destination leaf 5 and has no right on stack value 0. Results: drun isSome = true; MetaRightM = false; ReleasePre 6 = true; PushStatic = false. Then grow 0 6 1 (room: 1+1 ≤ 3) gives \"reject\"; grow 0 6 5 (no room) gives \"fail\"; push 0 5 6 (no room) gives \"reject\". theorem ce_grow_fail_masks_static : ... ∧ (dexec K0 (final opsZ) (.grow 0 6 1)).isReject = true ∧ (dexec K0 (final opsZ) (.grow 0 6 5)).isFail = true ∧ (dexec K0 (final opsZ) (.push 0 5 6)).isReject = true := by decide\n  VERDICT: I confirmed the behaviour. grow checks only `0 < extra`, `x.auth = b`, `x.base + x.size = st.top` and `ReleasePre` before its room test (DataStack.lean:198-199). MetaRightM is checked only later, inside IssueSeq/SplitPre (:200). In one reached state with no Modify on the stack value, the outcome therefore depends on the room. push checks MetaRightM before fail (:145, PushStatic :90-92), so the two operations are inconsistent, and grow does not follow the report's own rationale (f): 'Clauses statiques avant fail (§58), par analogie avec §77', since §77.1 includes MetaRight. I downgrade the severity from major to minor for three reasons. (1) The choice is reported: the grow docstring (:187-190) and report (f) list exactly the clauses checked before fail ('grow : 0 < extra, b dernier bloc et au sommet, b libérable'), so MetaRightM's absence is visible. (2) No normative text binds grow's order: §58 restricts the static-before-fail rule to alloc and issue 'dans ce noyau', and the brief says only 'fail si pas de place'. (3) What remains is a rationale that is applied inconsistently and not flagged, not an unreported semantic choice.\n  VEVIDENCE: I reran a copy of q2grow/B.lean in verify-grow/B.lean. Output: drun isSome = true; (MetaRightM, ReleasePre 6, PushStatic) = (false, true, false); grow 0 6 1 = \"reject\"; grow 0 6 5 = \"fail\"; push 0 5 6 = \"reject\". 'ce_grow_fail_masks_static' depends on axioms: [propext, Quot.sound]. verify-grow/G.lean shows the reject of grow 0 6 1 comes from the split of internal, not from store: on releaseEff s 6 with region [2,3], (MetaRightM, SplitPre, StorePre, Active internal) = (false, false, true, true). Spec §77.4: \"Une région indisponible n'autorise pas à contourner un rejet statique préalable.\" Spec §58: \"seules alloc et issue peuvent l'émettre dans ce noyau ... Pour alloc et issue, le contrôle des préconditions statiques précède la détermination de l'échec dynamique\".\n- Q2-DERIVED-NOT-RESTRICTED [major->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:87-108 (PushStatic, IssueSeq), :141-156 (push), :191-209 (grow); formal/reports/Derivability_v0.1.md (f) 'Décisions de représentation ... sans effet'\n  CLAIM: The brief says push issues at the top through the restricted issue ('issue restreint'). push and grow instead use the derived sequence split+store (IssueSeq). This drops the §77.2 'non Init' clause of the restricted issue, so push accepts an already-Init destination that IssueR rejects, and grow in place works only because of that. This changes which programs are accepted. AGENTS.md calls such a choice a semantic question, but the report files it under 'Décisions de représentation prises ici (sans effet sur la question posée)'. The docstrings of push and PushStatic do not say that the non-Init clause is missing. The report does note, in (d) and (e) 2, that the brief's grow conflicts with §77.2.\n  EVIDENCE: Spec §77.2: \"destination est une feuille utilisable, non Init\". Counterexamples, decided in scratch .../q2grow/E.lean and F.lean (axioms: propext, Quot.sound). (1) opsI := setup1 ++ [.kernel (.write 2 [5])]: I 5 = init; IssueStaticR on region [2] into 5 = false (it is true on final setup1, so only the Init clause separates them); dexec (.push 0 1 5) isOk = true. (2) theorem ce_grow_needs_init_dst: (dexec K0 (final opsA) (.grow 0 6 1)).isOk = true ∧ (releaseEff (final opsA).s 6).I 5 = InitSt.init ∧ IssueStaticR (releaseEff ..) st.arena [2,3,4] 5 = false, by decide.\n  VERDICT: The counterexamples reproduce. push and grow use split+store (IssueSeq), which has no §77.2 non-Init clause, so push accepts an Init destination that IssueStaticR rejects, and grow in place relies on this. The claim that this is unreported is wrong, so major is not justified. Report (f) says 'DataStack.push et grow utilisent l'issue dérivé, sémantique de store. Ils acceptent une destination Init, ce que grow exige.' Report (d) adds: 'grow réémet dans une destination déjà Init ... IssueR le rejetterait (écart 2)'. The module docstring (DataStack.lean:12-13) says push is 'the derived issue ... split ... then store', and its line 23-24 points to report (f) for the decisions the brief leaves open. The brief also names 'the derived issue/reset' as the basis of Q2, and a grow into the same destination cannot be built on IssueR at all; the report raises this conflict in (e) 2. What remains is a categorisation error: this item sits under 'Décisions de représentation prises ici (sans effet sur la question posée, à confirmer)'. It changes accepted programs, so by AGENTS.md ('A choice that does is a semantic question') it is not a representation choice. Also, PushStatic and push do not say in their docstrings that the non-Init clause is absent.\n  VEVIDENCE: I reran copies in verify-grow/E.lean and F.lean. E: drun opsI isSome = true; (IssueStaticR on [2] into 5, I 5 = init) = (false, true); without the write the same IssueStaticR = true; 'ce_push_init_dst' depends on axioms: [propext, Quot.sound]. F: 'ce_grow_needs_init_dst' depends on axioms: [propext, Quot.sound]. Spec §77.2: \"destination est une feuille utilisable, non Init\". Report line 198: \"### Décisions de représentation prises ici (sans effet sur la question posée, à confirmer)\". Report line 202 is the push/grow bullet quoted above.\n- Q2-GROW-SAME-BASE-WORDING [minor->minor] real=True loc=formal/Ern/Lib/DataStack.lean:530-545 (grow_same_base docstring and statement); report (d) row grow_same_base\n  CLAIM: The docstring says grow 'releases b' and gives a last block 'held by the same destination', and the report adds 'ancien bloc inactif'. The statement proves less. b is shown inactive only in the intermediate state `releaseEff d.s b`, not in the arrival state d'.s. 'Same destination' is shown only for the metadata field Blk.dst = x.dst, not for the kernel holder of the new authority. Both facts are true in the runs I checked, but neither is stated or proved.\n  EVIDENCE: Statement line 536: `st'.blocks.getLast? = some ⟨x.base, x.size + extra, d.s.A.length, x.dst⟩` (metadata only). Line 539: `(releaseEff d.s b).A[b]?.map (·.active) = some false` (intermediate state, not d'.s). No conjunct mentions `d'.s.A[d.s.A.length]?` holder or `d'.s.A[b]?`. Eval of final opsG (scratch D.lean): auth 9 = (true, [2,3,4], some 5, \"P5\"), auth 6 = (false, ...). So the claims hold in this run but are not part of the theorem.\n  VERDICT: Confirmed by reading the statement. The docstring says the new last block is 'held by the same destination'. The theorem shows only the metadata field (`st'.blocks.getLast? = some ⟨x.base, x.size + extra, d.s.A.length, x.dst⟩`), not the kernel holder of authority d.s.A.length in d'.s. §77 uses 'détenue par destination' for the kernel holder. The report's 'ancien bloc inactif' is proved only for the intermediate state `releaseEff d.s b`, not for d'.s. 'releases b' itself is accurately covered by the last conjunct. Both missing facts hold in case_grow, so this is a wording gap between the docstring/report and the statement: minor, as reported.\n  VEVIDENCE: DataStack.lean:536 `st'.blocks.getLast? = some ⟨x.base, x.size + extra, d.s.A.length, x.dst⟩` and :539 `(releaseEff d.s b).A[b]?.map (·.active) = some false`. No conjunct mentions d'.s.A[b]? or the holder of d'.s.A[d.s.A.length]?. verify-grow/G.lean on final opsG gives (A[6].active, A[9].holder) = (some false, some \"P5\"), so the facts hold in that run but are not stated. Spec §77 effect: \"elle est détenue par destination\".","numLines":198,"startLine":1,"totalLines":198}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8e3cc9de-00fa-4599-9697-327acb24e434","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"other"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"formal/derivability_0.1","slug":"effervescent-gathering-wind"}
